uations then samples challenge (serial ordering)\n\n4. **Column Openings Extraction**\n   - **Lines**: 651-672 in `logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n   - `prover.into_column_openings()` - Extract openings after all folding\n   - Observe column claims to transcript\n   - **Output**: column_openings\n   - **Transcript Dependencies**: Observes all opening claims\n\n#### **PHASE 3: Openings (Stacked Reduction + WHIR)**\n- **File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs`, line 134\n- **Function**: `prove_openings()`\n- **Calls**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, line 187\n- **Function**: `prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu()`\n\n**Sub-phases within Openings (in order):**\n\n1. **Stacked Reduction (Batch Sumcheck)**\n   - **Lines**: 187-272 in `stacked_reduction.rs`\n   - **Steps**:\n     - Sample λ for batching\n     - `prover.batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly()` - Univariate round 0 (same as constraints phase)\n       - Calls GPU kernel `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0()` per trace\n       - Reconstructs s_0 polynomial via NTT on CPU\n     - Observe s_0 coefficients to transcript\n     - Sample u_0 challenge\n     - `prover.fold_ple_evals()` - Fold evaluation matrices\n     - **MLE Sumcheck Rounds** (1..n_stack):\n       - `prover.batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` - Compute sumcheck evaluations\n       - Observe round evals to transcript\n       - Sample u_round challenge\n       - `prover.fold_mle_evals()` - Fold matrices\n     - `prover.get_stacked_openings()` - Extract stacked column openings\n     - Observe openings to transcript\n   - **Output**: StackingProof, u_prisma vector\n   - **Transcript Dependencies**: Sequential observation/sampling\n\n2. **WHIR Opening Proof**\n   - **File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`, line 62\n   - **Function**: `prove_whir_opening_gpu()`\n   - **Lines**: 62-500+\n   - **Steps**:\n     - `transcript.grind_gpu()` - Proof-of-work for batching soundness\n     - `transcript.sample_ext()` - Sample μ for algebraic batching\n     - Algebraic batching of stacked matrices (GPU kernel)\n     - MLE coefficient computation via iDFT + zeta transforms\n     - Compute moments of eq(u, -)\n     - **WHIR Rounds** (multiple rounds of tree building, folding, sumcheck):\n       - Per round: compute sumcheck poly, observe to transcript, sample challenge\n       - Generate Merkle proofs for codeword queries\n       - Fold polynomials based on sampled challenges\n   - **Output**: WhirProof (merkle proofs, sumcheck polys, etc.)\n   - **Transcript Dependencies**: Observes/samples in each WHIR round\n\n---\n\n### Data Dependencies Between Phases\n\n```\nTRACE COMMIT\n    ↓ (outputs: main_commitment, pcs_data)\n    \nCONSTRAINTS PHASE\n├─ GKR (LogUp) \n│   ├─ Input: interactions_layout, trace_data\n│   ├─ Output: gkr_proof\n│   └─ Transcript: reads (α_logup, β_logup), observes grind\n│\n├─ Round 0\n│   ├─ Input: gkr_proof outputs, trace matrices, common_main_pcs_data\n│   ├─ Output: s_0_poly, folded matrices\n│   └─ Transcript: reads λ, r_0\n│\n└─ MLE Rounds 1..n_max\n    ├─ Input: previous folded matrices, previous r value\n    ├─ Output: sumcheck_round_polys, column_openings\n    └─ Transcript: reads r_round per iteration (SERIAL)\n\nOPENINGS PHASE\n├─ Stacked Reduction\n│   ├─ Input: r vector (from constraints), common_main_pcs_data\n│   ├─ Output: stacking_proof, u_prisma\n│   └─ Transcript: reads u_round per round (SERIAL)\n│\n└─ WHIR\n    ├─ Input: u_prisma (from stacking), stacked matrices\n    ├─ Output: whir_proof\n    └─ Transcript: reads μ, challenge per whir_round (SERIAL)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Fiat-Shamir Transcript Usage (Critical Ordering Constraints)\n\nThe transcript creates **hard blocking** dependencies because challenges depend on observed data:\n\n1. **Constraints Phase**:\n   - Observe: main_commit, preprocessed_commits, cached_commits\n   - Observe: grind_witness (LogUp PoW)\n   - **Sample**: α_logup, β_logup\n   - Observe: sumcheck coefficients per round\n   - **Sample**: challenge per round (BLOCKS on observation)\n\n2. **Openings Phase**:\n   - Observe: grind_witness (WHIR PoW)\n   - **Sample**: μ (BLOCKS on grind)\n   - Observe: sumcheck coefficients per round\n   - **Sample**: challenge per round (BLOCKS on observation)\n\n---\n\n### Concurrency Analysis\n\n#### **Can Run Concurrently:**\n\n1. **Within Round 0 Processing** (logup_zerocheck, line 100):\n   - `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS = 8` threads process different AIRs in parallel\n   - Each thread calls `process_air_round0()` independently\n   - **Dependency**: All must complete before CPU-side batching\n   - **Bottleneck**: Synchronization point after GPU work\n\n2. **GKR Precompute-M Phase** (fractional.rs, lines 144-150):\n   - Large N: \"PrecomputeM\" strategy can be parallelized within GPU\n   - Tiled tail computation with configurable block/tile parameters\n\n3. **Within Stacked Reduction MLE Rounds**:\n   - `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` processes multiple commits in parallel\n   - Kernel-level parallelism (all GPU work is intrinsically parallel)\n\n#### **Cannot Overlap (Hard Dependencies):**\n\n1. **Phase Sequencing**: Constraints MUST complete before Openings\n   - Stacked Reduction uses `r` vector from Constraints\n   - WHIR uses `u_prisma` vector from Stacked Reduction\n\n2. **Round-to-Round within Phases**:\n   - Each MLE round depends on:\n     - Previous fold results (matrix data)\n     - Sampled challenge from transcript (BLOCKS on previous observation)\n   - Cannot pipeline: Round i+1 challenge depends on Round i observation\n\n3. **Transcript Synchronization**:\n   - Every challenge sample blocks waiting for prior observation\n   - No way to overlap with transcript-dependent branching\n\n---\n\n### Performance-Critical Path (1312ms breakdown estimate)\n\nBased on code analysis, the main contributions are likely:\n\n1. **LogUp GKR** (~300-400ms estimated)\n   - `fractional_sumcheck_gpu()` with multiple tree layers\n   - Compute+fold rounds with complex kernel work\n\n2. **Round 0 across all phases** (~200-300ms estimated)\n   - GPU kernel execution per AIR (8-parallel streams)\n   - NTT operations for polynomial reconstruction\n   - CPU interpolation overhead\n\n3. **MLE Rounds 1..n_max** (~300-400ms estimated)\n   - Per-round overhead: GPU kernel + CPU polynomial evaluation\n   - Sequential sampling creates idle time waiting for challenges\n\n4. **Stacked Reduction** (~150-200ms estimated)\n   - Similar structure to Constraints phases\n   - Smaller n_stack usually means fewer rounds\n\n5. **WHIR Rounds** (~150-250ms estimated)\n   - Multiple tree folding rounds\n   - Merkle proof queries and RS codeword operations\n\n---\n\n### Overlap Opportunities\n\n**Limited overlap possible due to Fiat-Shamir transcript ordering:**\n\n1. **Pre-computation before sampling**:\n   - Round i+1 can pre-compute intermediate values while Round i samples challenge\n   - Example: Build fold-invariant matrices for next round\n   - **Gain**: ~5-10% if Round i observation→sample is on critical path\n\n2. **Kernel pipelining within GPU**:\n   - Multiple small kernels in Round i+1 can start while Round i kernel finishes\n   - **Gain**: Marginal (GPU already well-utilized)\n\n3. **GKR and Round 0 parallel initialization**:\n   - While GKR computes, could start Round 0 kernel launches\n   - **Constraint**: Both need common data structures (traces)\n   - **Gain**: ~50-100ms if GKR is not data-dependent\n\n4. **Stacked Reduction and WHIR kernel overlap**:\n   - Algebraic batching (line 128 in whir.rs) could start while Stacked Reduction samples\n   - **Gain**: ~20-50ms per round\n\n**Key blocker**: Every phase transitions rely on challenges from transcript, which are strictly ordered. No parallelism across phase boundaries.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Tables\n\n**Call Chain by Function**:\n\n| File | Function | Line | Parent | Output |\n|------|----------|------|--------|--------|\n| `prover/mod.rs` | `Coordin