hread kernel with a work queue — this eliminates launch overhead and enables dynamic load balancing, potentially saving 10-20ms, though it's complex to implement.\n\nStepping back though, I need 222ms of reduction to hit 2x scaling from APC 0 to APC 300, bringing the 1312ms down to 1090ms.\n\nLooking at where the scaling breaks down, I'm examining the component-by-component performance across the two configurations to identify which parts are dragging down the overall speedup.\n\nThe real culprits are MLE Rounds, which actually gets slower instead of faster, Round 0 that doesn't scale at all, and Stacked Reduction that stays flat—together these account for 242ms of the gap. If I could fix these three components to scale properly, APC 300 would hit around 1070ms instead of 1312ms.\n\nThe problem is that Round 0 and Stacked Reduction have fixed per-AIR overhead that doesn't shrink when there's less work per AIR, and APC 300 has way more AIRs (623 vs 99) but less work in each one. The MLE Rounds anti-scaling is the biggest issue though—it goes from 114ms to 172ms when it should drop to 57ms. I need to dig into why the per-AIR MLE cost is increasing, whether it's the monomial evaluation kernels, the batch MLE evaluation, or the interpolation that's causing the slowdown.\n\nLooking at the kernel launch counts, I can see the batch MLE kernels run once per round (24 and 45 instances), but the monomial kernels are launching way more times (178 instances each), which suggests they're running per-AIR per-round. The interpolation kernel has 93 instances, and there are more kernels in the trace that got cut off. The number of MLE rounds itself depends on the trace height, which is smaller at APC 300, but that's being overwhelmed by having 6x more AIRs to process.\n\nNow looking at the actual timing data, the monomial kernels are where the per-AIR overhead really shows up. The zerocheck monomial kernel is averaging 93 microseconds per instance, while the parallel version is much