emory access patterns. The partition structures might already be organizing this efficiently, but it's worth checking whether pre-packing the partition data into contiguous memory could improve cache locality and reduce memory bandwidth pressure.\n\nI think the descriptor-array batching approach for MLE Round monomial evaluations is the most practical choice—it's a proven pattern in the codebase and targets a clear scaling bottleneck. Even though 15-25ms on 1312ms total is only 1.1-1.9%, it's a moderate-complexity change with predictable results, which makes it worth pursuing over more speculative optimizations.\n\nBut let me reconsider the bigger picture. Trace Commit is taking 247ms, and if I could shave off 30-50ms there, that'd be 2.3-3.8% improvement on the STARK time. The challenge is that most of the work is intrinsic—the Poseidon2 hashing, the NTT operations for the RS code matrix, and the Merkle tree construction are all fundamental to the algorithm. Looking at the breakdown, the NTT operations alone account for around 112ms, and the row hashing in the Merkle tree is another 150ms, so there's not much slack to find without rethinking the approach entirely. K columns), hashing a compressed representation instead? That could reduce the hash input size significantly. Or maybe use a tree-based hash where we hash row segments and then combine them, trading some parallelism for reduced computation per row.\n\nBut wait—changing the hash structure would alter the commitment scheme itself, which isn't an option here.\n\nSo Trace Commit seems pretty locked in at this performance level without protocol changes. Let me shift focus and examine where the NTT operations are spending their time across the different phases.\n\nLooking at the NTT kernels, the forward and inverse transforms are taking up significant GPU cycles, along with bit reversal and batch operations. These show up in trace commit, MLE rounds, stacked reduction, and WHIR—totaling around 177ms of GPU time. The challenge is that NTT is fundamental to the proof system, so I can't just eliminate it.\n\nNow I need to decide which optimization to pursue. I'm weighing three main candidates: batching the MLE monomial evaluations for 15-25ms of gains, increasing the GKR input evaluation threads to 16 for 20-40ms, or parallelizing trace commit operations across commit groups for another 20-40ms. The thread increase seems promising since I've already seen gains from scaling up before.\n\nActually, looking back at the optimization history, the 4→8 thread increase for GKR input eval only yielded 14ms, which suggests diminishing returns. But I need to reconsider the timeline—that improvement happened before the buffer pre-allocation was added. Once pre-allocation was in place, the mutex contention that was limiting the earlier gains got resolved. So pushing to 16 threads now might actually be more effective than it would have been back then.\n\nThe real constraint is whether the GPU can handle 16 concurrent streams. The RTX 4090 has 128 SMs, and with 16 streams each launching 256-block kernels, that's 4096 concurrent blocks total—about 32 waves, which the GPU can definitely manage. Even the large AIR kernels at 256 blocks each would fit comfortably. The question becomes whether smaller AIRs would still get enough resources.\n\nIf the GKR input eval segment is currently around 300ms with 8 threads, scaling to 16 threads with pre-allocated buffers could potentially cut that to 200ms—a 33% improvement that would add roughly 100ms to the overall STARK performance, or about 7.6% gain. But I need to figure out whether the bottleneck is actually insufficient stream concurrency or something else like GPU compute saturation, since the previous jump from 4 to 8 threads only yielded 14ms improvement.\n\nSmall AIRs could benefit from more threads since they launch fewer blocks and finish quickly, allowing better overlap with other streams. Large AIRs that already launch 256 blocks and occupy many SMs probably won't see much benefit. At APC 300, seg0 has a mix of both, and the wall-clock time is likely dominated by the large AIRs that can't be parallelized further anyway.\n\nSo maybe the real win is splitting the large AIR GKR input eval across multiple threads instead of just adding more threads overall.\n\nFor large AIRs with TASK_SIZE = 65536 elements, I could split the work across 2-4 workers, each processing a sub-range of the elements independently. Since the kernel evaluates interaction expressions at each element and writes to a per-element output buffer, splitting is straightforward—each worker just processes a different range and writes to the appropriate offset in the output buffer. A 13ms kernel split across 4 streams would complete in roughly 3.25ms. The kernel already runs with 256 blocks, so splitting into 4 sub-ranges gives each sub-range 64 blocks that can run concurrently on the 128 SMs, meaning the sub-kernels would finish in about the same time as a single 64-block kernel.\n\nActually, if the GPU can already run all 256 blocks concurrently (which it likely can, since 128 SMs × 2 blocks/SM = 256 blocks), then the full kernel is already running at peak parallelism in a single wave. Splitting doesn't help because the GPU is already fully utilized. The real bottleneck for large AIRs is the actual compute time itself, not stream-level parallelism. The only way to speed things up is to optimize the kernel directly—improve the interaction DAG evaluation, refine memory access patterns, or use shared memory more effectively.\n\nBut let me think about the `observe_and_update()` function in the fractional sumcheck. There's a D2H copy that drains the GPU pipeline, and then the CPU does transcript work before launching the next kernel. The gap between kernels includes the copy time, CPU processing, and launch overhead. If I could overlap these with async copies and callbacks, I could keep the GPU pipeline flowing while the CPU prepares the next step.\n\nHowever, the compute kernel is only around 500 microseconds for most rounds, and the D2H transfer is just 32 bytes—essentially negligible once the kernel finishes. The overlap window is too small to matter. The fractional sumcheck is inherently sequential, and the GPU work per round is already so small that the overhead between rounds isn't the bottleneck.\n\nI think I've analyzed this enough. Let me settle on my best approach and write out the plan.\n\nActually, wait—let me step back and think about what would really move the needle here.\n\nLogUp GKR at 534ms keeps standing out as the obvious target. It's the largest single component and has the most potential for improvement. Looking at the breakdown: input evaluation takes about 300ms and is constrained by how fast the large AIR kernels can execute, while the fractional sumcheck is around 172ms and limited by the sequential nature of the protocol. The input eval has already been heavily optimized with multi-streaming, so the remaining time is just the kernel execution itself—I can't improve that without making the kernel faster. For the fractional sumcheck, the 172ms comes from initial tree builds, the core round computation kernels, and the precomputation and multifold operations.\n\nWhen I add up the GPU time across these operations, I'm getting around 230ms, but the wall-clock time is only 172ms. That gap suggests either some parallelism between CPU and GPU work, or some of these kernels are actually part of the input eval phase rather than the fractional sumcheck. The tree building is definitely part of the fractional sumcheck initialization, so all that GPU time should be accounted for there. The discrepancy between GPU time and wall-clock time probably means CPU transcript processing is overlapping with GPU kernel execution, or the GPU profiling is picking up work from other fractional sumchecks in the proof.\n\nEither way, the fractional sumcheck is fundamentally bounded by kernel execution and the sequential protocol rounds—there's not much room to optimize further.\n\nSo here's what I'm settling on: the best target is batching the per-AIR MLE round monomial evaluations together with the precompute_lambda_combinations kernels using descriptor-array batched kernel launches. The MLE Rounds are scaling poorly from 114ms up to 172ms as we go from APC 0 to APC 300, which accounts for 58ms of the gap, and those non-batched per-AIR kernels are burning through 44ms across over 1400 launches. Batching eliminates launch overhead, improves SM utilization for these tiny kernels, and reduces CPU orchestration time—this pattern is already proven in the codebase. I'd expect to recover 20-35ms on MLE Rounds, which would bring APC 300 much closer to where it should be.\n\nBut now I'm second-guessing whether 25-40ms improvement on a 1312ms baseline is actually worth it—that's only 1.9-3.0%, which feels thin. Let me look at what's actually been considered successful before. The batch-mle-round-kernels work didn't have an explicit threshold but was treated as a win, batch-mle-interpolation got 15ms and that was good, but batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload only got 5ms and that was considered a failure. For something moderately complex, I think I need at least 20-25ms on the main STARK metric to justify the effort.\n\nSo maybe I should think bigger—what if I combined multiple small optimizations together? The monomial batching and lambda batching could work in the same task, and there might be other per-AIR kernel overhead I could tackle simultaneously. Let me map out all the per-AIR kernel launches happening in MLE Rounds to see where the real time is going.\n\nLooking at the kernel breakdown, I'm seeing that kernels 1-5 account for about 44.3ms of GPU time across roughly 1387 launches. But there's also the CPU-side cost—with around 17 MLE rounds, each one needs to prepare parameters, launch kernels, copy results, and update the transcript. If each round involves 80+ kernel launches,