de lives in its own function in a separate module, so the optimizer interference might not carry over. That said, when they tried moving batched code to a separate module with `#[inline(never)]`, it still ran into problems—though it was OOM in the GKR context rather than the optimizer issue itself.\n\nFor GKR input eval, I'd create a separate batched module that builds descriptor arrays for all small AIRs, launches a single batched kernel for those, handles large AIRs individually, and merges the results. This would require a new CUDA kernel accepting descriptor arrays and corresponding Rust code to build and launch them. The potential gain is 20-40ms on GKR input eval, but the risk is medium—there's still a chance of hitting Rust optimizer or CUDA issues, and the complexity is substantial.\n\nNow I'm comparing this against other optimization options. Looking at the expected value across different approaches, batching GKR input eval comes out ahead with 10-20ms expected improvement, outperforming simpler alternatives like increasing thread count or optimizing the WHIR phase. I'm noticing something interesting in the data that might change the analysis.\n\nActually, the monomial evaluations are already batched using descriptor arrays, so the real bottleneck is the CPU-side work: constructing those descriptor arrays and transferring them to the GPU. Breaking down the 172ms MLE Rounds time, about 111ms is GPU computation while 61ms is CPU overhead—mostly from building the BlockCtx and MonomialAirCtx structures per AIR, uploading the descriptors, and copying results back. I'm wondering if there's room to reduce the descriptor construction cost, especially since the AIR layout stays consistent across rounds.\n\nThe trace data gets folded between MLE rounds, which changes the pointers and sizes in the descriptors, but the structural information—which AIRs exist, how many monomials per AIR, which variables—remains constant. So I could split the descriptor setup into a one-time structural initialization before the first round and then just update the data pointers each subsequent round, which would save the repeated structural construction work.\n\nLooking at where this actually happens though, descriptor construction occurs within each batch evaluation call, and there are about 4 batch evaluations per round. The structural part is mainly iterating over AIRs and building the BlockCtx and MonomialAirCtx objects. If I precompute the BlockCtx once and reuse it, that's maybe a 30% savings on descriptor construction—around 6ms per round. Not really worth the added complexity.\n\nI'm going to move forward with the most promising optimization instead.\n\nMy final approach is batching GKR input evaluation for small AIRs using a descriptor-array CUDA kernel. This targets the biggest bottleneck (GKR input eval at ~300ms for seg0) by reducing per-AIR kernel launch overhead and improving GPU utilization across many small AIRs. I'll define a descriptor struct for each AIR's trace pointers and parameters, create a batched kernel where each block handles one small AIR, partition AIRs by size in the worker function, launch one batched kernel for all small AIRs and individual kernels for large ones, then merge the results. The main risk is Rust optimizer interference from the previous batch attempt, so I'll isolate this in a separate module.\n\nThe real benefit isn't just launch overhead—it's GPU SM utilization. Small AIRs currently launch only 4-16 blocks each, leaving most of the GPU's 128 SMs idle. With 8 concurrent streams, I could theoretically get 128 blocks running simultaneously, but only if all streams have pending work. A single batched kernel processes all small AIRs from one thread together, which should pack the GPU much more efficiently.\n\nFor seg0, that's roughly 30 small AIRs per thread, which translates to around 300 blocks per thread or 2400 blocks across all 8 threads—enough to fully saturate the GPU. Without batching, the separate