he kernels from different streams might not be overlapping perfectly.\n\nIf I divide the total kernel time by the number of streams, I'd expect an ideal wall-clock of about 66ms, but I'm seeing 362ms instead—that's a 5.5x overhead factor. This means only about 1.45 streams are active on average, so the GPU is severely underutilized at just 18% capacity.\n\nThe culprit is likely that large AIR kernels are blocking smaller ones from overlapping, or there's significant CPU overhead between launches. But the real issue might be that each thread has pre-allocated buffers it uses serially—it can't launch the next kernel until the current one finishes using the buffer.\n\nI thought pre-allocation would help by avoiding mutex-protected allocations, but it actually serializes kernels within each thread. With cudaStreamPerThread, kernels on the same stream run in order, so multi-stream benefits only come from inter-thread overlap. With 8 threads, the GPU sees 8 serial streams instead of true parallelism.\n\nThe GPU hardware does overlap kernels from different streams—blocks from all active streams get scheduled across SMs. But each thread serially submits kernels since they share a buffer, so the CPU-side submission rate becomes the bottleneck. If each thread takes 1ms to prepare and launch a kernel, 8 threads can submit 8 kernels per millisecond, which over 362ms would be around 2896 submissions—but that's likely not enough to saturate the GPU.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers: with only 791 instances and 8 threads, each thread submits roughly 99 kernels, taking about 99ms of CPU time. The remaining 263ms is GPU execution time. This suggests the wall-clock time is dominated by whichever thread has the most work, likely seg0. For seg0 specifically, I'm estimating around 39 AIRs per thread with roughly 1ms CPU prep per AIR and variable GPU times (0.2-13ms), but the exact breakdown gets complicated with multiple segments. I should focus on the optimization plan instead—batching