o with the simpler approach: increase the GKR input eval thread count to 16 with interleaved distribution. If it doesn't hit the target, at least I'll have concrete data.\n\nWait—I just realized something much more impactful. The fractional sumcheck has a significant D2H copy and transcript processing step that's happening sequentially. What if I fuse that into the GPU pipeline instead?\n\nRight now each of the 141 rounds does a synchronous copy to host, computes a Poseidon2 hash for the transcript, then launches the next kernel. That synchronization drains the entire pipeline and creates a ~20us gap per round—adding up to nearly 3ms of wasted time across all rounds.\n\nBut if the host-side work is more involved than just copying, like allocating memory or processing results, that gap could balloon to 50-100us per round, pushing the total overhead to 7-14ms. Still not huge, but it's worth considering.\n\nThe real bottleneck isn't these per-round synchronizations though—it's the actual kernel execution time for the fractional sumcheck itself.\n\nI'm thinking about restructuring this to pipeline the logup precompute and Round 0 evaluation for the next segment while the current segment's fractional sumcheck is still running. But wait, segments are processed sequentially, so seg1 hasn't even started when seg0's fractional sumcheck is happening. We can't prefetch seg1's data that way.\n\nWhat if I reorganized the loop entirely—compute all segments' GKR input evaluations first, then run all their fractional sumchecks afterward?\n\nRestructuring the loop...\n\nActually, each segment produces its own independent proof with different AIR instances and traces, so there might be room to overlap different segments' phases.\n\nIf I process segment 0's fractional sumcheck concurrently with segment 1's GKR input eval on separate GPU streams, I could hide some latency. Segment 1's input eval takes 62ms while segment 0's fractional sumcheck takes 116ms, so it runs essentially for free during that window. Without overlapping, the total is around 885ms, but with careful scheduling I can compress it down to roughly 240 + 116 + 90 + 86 by interleaving the independent work across both segments.\n\nThe challenge is GPU contention—both segments are competing for the same hardware. I need to verify whether the fractional sumcheck actually has enough idle GPU periods to accommodate segment 1's input eval without significant slowdown, since that's where the real savings come from. I'm working through the timeline more carefully now—seg0 r0 actually finishes at t=470, not t=370, which means seg0 mle can't start until then. The overlapping phases with GPU contention are making the dependencies tricky to track. me draw it out with a timeline showing how segments can overlap. Seg0 finishes its input phase at 240ms, then seg1 starts its input while seg0 moves into the fractional phase. By interleaving their work across the GPU, both segments complete by 675ms instead of 885ms sequentially—that's a 210ms savings, or about 16% of the total STARK time even accounting for GPU contention overhead.\n\nThe real question is whether this is architecturally feasible. Each segment maintains its own Fiat-Shamir transcript, so I need to verify they're truly independent. There's also the matter of GPU memory—both segments' data need to be in VRAM simultaneously. And managing the thread pools becomes more complex when each segment has its own multi-stream phases running in parallel.\n\nThe memory constraint is the critical blocker here. One segment currently peaks around 15GB, so two concurrent segments would need roughly 30GB, but we only have 24GB available. Unless the segments can share most of their data structures, this won't work. The trace data is segment-specific, but the preprocessed data is shared, so maybe each segment only needs 10-12GB at peak, bringing the total to 20-24GB—a tight fit that risks out-of-memory errors if we're not careful.\n\nBeyond the memory issue, the architectural change itself is substantial. We'd need to completely restructure the proof loop from a simple sequential iteration into an overlapped execution model, which introduces significant complexity and correctness risks around shared state management.\n\nThe potential gain is substantial—150-210ms improvement—but the probability of success is low to medium given the memory constraints and implementation complexity. This is too risky for the auto-optimization loop, which needs reliable, achievable tasks. I'm going to set this aside and look for a simpler, more dependable optimization instead.\n\nI'm settling on reducing GKR input evaluation overhead by switching to round-robin distribution and bumping the stream count from 8 to 16. Round-robin alone only saved 8ms before, but that was with fewer threads—with 16 threads, the finer granularity should improve load balancing more effectively. I'm expecting 15-30ms of improvement, and the implementation is trivial—just two line changes. Even if it doesn't hit the full 30ms threshold, the data will be useful, and it's easy to revert if needed.\n\nLooking at the historical rollback criteria, I'm noticing a pattern: batch-mle-interpolation hit 15ms against a 10ms threshold and succeeded, while others fell short. For this task, 10% of the 302ms GKR input eval phase gives me a 30ms target, which aligns with my expected range. But I'm second-guessing whether this is the best approach—let me pivot and examine the Trace Commit phase more carefully instead. It's sitting at 247ms, and the stack traces portion is only 16ms, so there might be a quicker win hiding elsewhere.\n\nThe real bottleneck is the Merkle tree step at 166ms, but before that, the RS code phase takes about 65ms doing MLE interpolation and NTT sequentially. I'm wondering if I can pipeline the NTT computation with the row hashing that comes after—start hashing rows from the first column batch while still processing NTT on the remaining columns. The problem is that row hashing needs all columns complete before it can begin, so this approach won't work unless I fundamentally restructure how rows are processed.\n\nI'm also considering whether the Poseidon2 sponge could absorb columns incrementally as they become available, but that would require storing intermediate sponge states and breaks the normal processing order—probably not worth the complexity.\n\nLet me look at the NTT kernel itself. The batch_ntt uses a radix-8 kernel that's taking 82ms across 50 instances at APC 300. Could switching to a larger radix like radix-16 help reduce the number of stages and improve throughput?\n\nThe issue is that the NTT kernel comes from Plonky3's p3-ntt crate as a git dependency, so I can't easily modify it. Even if the kernel code exists in the cuda-backend files, optimizing Plonky3 itself is outside the scope of what I can do here.\n\nSo I'm settling on the simplest optimization with reasonable upside: increasing NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS from 8 to 16 and switching to round-robin chunk distribution. It's a couple-line change that could yield 15-30ms improvement with decent odds, and if it doesn't work, at least I'll understand the multi-streaming limits better. Time to write this up and stop second-guessing myself.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4892,"cache_read_input_tokens":153396,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4892},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4314f39-e91d-47fe-87bd-a8908ed81e1e","uuid":"396fa423-5b4c-4854-94ab-420e6f0df04a"}
