ation, and the time varies significantly between GLOBAL AIRs (around 100us) and SCATTER AIRs (around 10us). I initially thought the batched kernel would process different AIRs' blocks sequentially, but actually the GPU scheduler handles this more efficiently—blocks from small AIRs finish quickly and free up SMs for the next batch. The total GPU time would be dominated by the longest-running blocks rather than a simple sum.\n\nLooking at this from a work perspective: the total single-SM-equivalent work is 527ms, so with 128 SMs the theoretical minimum is about 4.1ms. But we're seeing 302ms wall-clock time, which is 73x slower than ideal. The main bottleneck is that each GLOBAL kernel launches 256 blocks across all 128 SMs simultaneously, creating scheduling constraints and preventing efficient pipelining between different AIRs.\n\nIf I batch all AIRs into a single kernel, the GPU can interleave their blocks more flexibly—small SCATTER blocks can fill gaps while GLOBAL blocks execute, keeping utilization near 100%. But memory bandwidth is likely the real constraint here. The RTX 4090 has about 1TB/s bandwidth, and if each AIR reads roughly 100 columns worth of data per element, I need to calculate whether we're actually hitting that limit or if it's something else.\n\nI'm overcomplicating the performance analysis. Let me shift focus to the actual implementation: I need to design a descriptor struct that holds per-AIR metadata like rules, node indices, partition pointers, and output buffers, then build the batched kernel around that.\n\nThe main challenge is handling the intermediates buffers—they vary wildly in size across AIRs depending on whether we're in GLOBAL or SCATTER mode. Pre-allocating one large buffer and carving out per-AIR regions could work, but the simpler approach is to only batch the SCATTER-mode AIRs where buffer sizes stay small.\n\nLooking at the APC 300 numbers, SCATTER AIRs account for about 305 kernels across both segments, but they only represent 63ms of the total 527ms runtime. Batching just those would eliminate the per-AIR overhead, but the overall speedup is modest. A better strategy is to batch everything—both GLOBAL and SCATTER—by pre-computing the total intermediates buffer size needed, allocating it upfront, and storing per-AIR offsets in the descriptor.\n\nThe challenge is that with 256 concurrent blocks across 128 SMs, each block needs its own intermediates buffer since threads within a block share row processing. This means the total memory required scales with concurrent_blocks × buffer_size × thread_count × element size, which could be substantial at scale.\n\nHowever, I'm realizing the key insight: in a batched kernel where each AIR has its own intermediates buffer, the task_stride is only gridDim.x × blockDim.x for that specific AIR, not the entire kernel. So the buffer size remains manageable even with many concurrent blocks, since each AIR's buffer is sized independently based on its own grid dimensions. memory limits but significantly more than the current 800MB approach where each thread pre-allocates its own buffer. The batching strategy would require all intermediates to be resident simultaneously, which could be problematic. I'm thinking the 95th-percentile approach might work better—allocate a shared buffer at that threshold size and only batch AIRs that fit within it, falling back to individual launches for larger ones. Let me focus on a simpler plan: batch only SCATTER-mode AIRs into a single kernel launch per worker thread.\n\nSince SCATTER AIRs use local intermediates arrays and don't need global buffers, I can avoid the memory complexity entirely. For each worker thread, I'd build a descriptor array for all assigned SCATTER AIRs and launch one batched kernel, then handle GLOBAL AIRs individually as before. This eliminates roughly 38 kernel launches per thread across both segments, though the actual launch overhead savings is minimal at around 0.38ms per thread. The real benefit is improving GPU utilization by batching those small SCATTER blocks together.\n\nBut wait—the total expected savings is only 5-10ms, which isn't worth the added complexity. Let me reconsider and batch ALL AIRs instead, both GLOBAL and SCATTER together.\n\nFor this to work, I need to rethink how the intermediates buffer is handled. In a batched kernel where each block processes a tile of one AIR's rows, GLOBAL-mode AIRs would need enough intermediates space per block to handle the buffer size times the block size, since threads within a block work on adjacent rows with the same stride.\n\nExamining the kernel structure...\n\nCalculating intermediates buffer layout... I need to adjust the intermediates indexing to use per-AIR offsets in the batched kernel, which adds some complexity but is manageable. The total memory across all 486 GLOBAL AIRs comes to around 156MB, which is acceptable, though individual AIRs could consume up to 200MB each if they have large buffer sizes and thread counts, so I need to watch for memory constraints when combining all AIRs.\n\nBatching all 791 kernel launches into roughly 2 launches per segment would save about 8ms in kernel launch overhead and 20ms in CPU preparation time, though only 2.5ms of that is on the critical path. The real win is the GPU scheduling improvement from having a single batched kernel, but given how complex the CUDA kernel changes and Rust-side orchestration would be, I should verify this improvement justifies the effort before committing to the implementation. SM utilization\n\nThe most important benefit: the GPU scheduler can now distribute ALL AIRs' blocks across SMs optimally, rather than launching them one-at-a-time on each stream. This could reduce the wall-clock from 302ms to closer to 527ms/8 = 66ms (ideal 8-stream parallel) or even better with batched scheduling.\n\nBut the 302ms wall-clock is already quite close to the 527ms/8 = 66ms ideal... no wait, 302/66 = 4.6x overhead. That's significant.\n\nActually, the 527ms GPU time is the SUM of all kernel durations. With 8 streams, the ideal wall-clock is 527/8 = 66ms only if all 8 streams' kernels overlap perfectly. In practice, large kernels (13ms each) prevent overlap.\n\nWith a batched kernel, all blocks pool together and process in waves of ~256 concurrent blocks. With roughly 127K blocks total, that's about 496 waves where most take ~100us but a few with large AIR blocks take ~1ms, giving me a rough estimate of 136ms per segment or around 100ms for seg0 alone—a potential 142ms improvement from the current 242ms. Even being conservative and assuming only 50% of that theoretical gain materializes, I'd expect roughly 50-100ms total improvement, which would be meaningful.\n\nThis justifies exploring a simpler approach: batch just the SCATTER-mode AIRs (those with small buffers) into a single kernel on the main thread before distributing the GLOBAL-mode AIRs across the 8 worker threads. This eliminates ~305 individual kernel launches while avoiding the full complexity of batching everything, and lets the GPU scheduler optimize block distribution across SMs more effectively. SCATTER AIRs no longer occupy multi-stream threads (freeing them for GLOBAL AIRs)\n- GLOBAL AIRs get better thread distribution (fewer AIRs per thread → less imbalance)\n\nThe SCATTER batched kernel needs:\n- Per-AIR descriptor struct (pointers to rules, used_nodes, pair_idxs, output, etc.)\n- Local intermediates array (buffer_size <= 10, fits in local memory)\n- No global intermediates buffer needed\n\nThis avoids the complex intermediates buffer management. It's also lower risk because the multi-stream GLOBAL path is unchanged.\n\nExpected improvement:\n- SCATTER AIRs currently take ~63ms GPU time across both segments\n- With batching, the SCATTER kernel runs as one launch: ~63ms total (same GPU time, but no launch overhead and better SM utilization)\n- The multi-stream GLOBAL path is improved because each thread has fewer AIRs (~20 for seg0 with round-robin)\n- Total improvement: ~20-40ms\n\nBut I realize the SCATTER kernel (63ms) runs before the multi-stream GLOBAL phase, which adds to total time unless they overlap. I could actually run them concurrently—the SCATTER batch on the default stream while GLOBAL AIRs run on per-thread streams. With ~150 blocks for SCATTER and 256 blocks for GLOBAL, both can share the 128 SMs, so the SCATTER batch would effectively run in ~31ms per segment while GLOBAL processing continues, reducing the overall wall-clock time significantly.\n\nThe real win is that GLOBAL threads now process only GLOBAL AIRs (~20 per thread instead of ~39), which shortens their per-thread wall time and improves load balance across the multi-stream execution. current per-thread wall time for the critical thread is ~242ms (includes both GLOBAL and SCATTER AIRs). If we remove the SCATTER AIRs, the critical thread only processes ~20 GLOBAL AIRs. The per-thread GPU time: ~350ms GLOBAL / 8 = ~44ms. Plus CPU overhead: ~20 × 0.5ms = ~10ms. Total: ~54ms per thread.\n\nWait, but the SCATTER batch runs concurrently: 31ms. Total wall-clock: max(54, 31) = 54ms per segment.\n\nvs current: 242ms for seg0. Improvement: 188ms!\n\nThat can't be right. There must be an error in my reasoning.\n\nThe issue: the 350ms GLOBAL GPU time is across both segments. For seg0 only, it's ~250ms. With 8 threads: 250/8 = ~31ms per thread IF perfectly balanced.\n\nBut contiguous chunking by height creates a bottleneck—thread 0 gets the 20 largest GLOBAL AIRs with kernel times of 5-13ms each, potentially hitting 100ms total if the average is 5ms. Round-robin distribution would be more balanced, spreading the AIRs evenly across threads and keeping per-thread GPU time closer to ~162 AIRs × 2ms average.\n\nSo the realistic improvement with round-robin threading is from 242ms down to around 50ms per segment, a 4.8× speedup. -192ms for seg0 alone!\n\nThat seems too good. Let me check my assumptions.\n\nWait, I think my assumption about removing SCATTER AIRs from threads is correct — they'd be processed in the separate batch kernel instead. So the threads ONLY need to handle GLOBAL AIRs. With fewer AIRs per thread, the critical path improves.\n\nBut the 242ms current wall-clock includes BOTH GLOBAL and SCATTER AIRs processed on 8 threads. If SCATTER AIRs account for ~40ms per thread (in the current contiguous scheme), removing them would save ~40ms per thread. Critical thread: 242ms → ~200ms. The SCATTER batch (31ms) runs concurrently. Total: 200ms.\n\nHmm, that's only 42ms savings. Not 192ms.\n\nThe discrepancy is because I was using ideal parallelism estimates earlier. In reality, with 8 threads and contiguous chunking, the critical thread takes much longer than ideal. So separating SCATTER AIRs would give a more modest improvement: the batched kernel handles SCATTER work efficiently in ~31ms while running concurrently, and GLOBAL threads with fewer AIRs would see a 30-50ms reduction in wall-clock time. That's roughly 30-50ms total improvement on GKR input eval, or 50-80ms across both segments—a solid 3.8-6.1% win on the STARK excluding trace. The implementation is straightforward too: I just need a new CUDA kernel for batched SCATTER-mode processing. I'm working through the GPU timing math for the batched SCATTER kernel. With 3050 blocks across 128 SMs, I'd get roughly 12 waves at ~100us each for a total of 1.2ms, but that seems too optimistic. The original per-AIR kernels ran at ~206us median, so accounting for the batch overhead and SM utilization, I'm estimating closer to 500us total, which is still a significant speedup from the original 8ms wall-clock time across concurrent streams.\n\nThe real win here is that SCATTER operations are currently interleaved with GLOBAL operations and don't add to wall-clock time, but batching them separately could actually reduce overall latency by eliminating the scheduling overhead and improving cache locality. The SCATTER AIRs might not actually impact the critical path since they're filling gaps between GLOBAL kernels anyway. Let me reconsider the actual timeline on a single thread—I need to trace through what happens when GLOBAL and SCATTER kernels interleave, accounting for CPU prep time between each kernel launch and execution.\n\nIf I remove a SCATTER AIR entirely, I'm saving about 150 microseconds per occurrence (25us CPU prep + 100us GPU execution + 25us CPU prep), which across 19 SCATTER AIRs per thread adds up to roughly 3 milliseconds per thread. With 8 threads, that's maybe a 3 millisecond wall-clock improvement total—essentially negligible compared to the overall execution time. The complexity of batching SCATTER operations doesn't justify such a marginal gain.\n\nThe real win from batching comes from consolidating all AIRs into a single kernel where the GPU can schedule blocks optimally across all streams at once. Right now, each thread launches kernels sequentially on its own stream, and while the GPU can overlap work from different streams, large kernels block SMs and prevent smaller kernels from running efficiently. A single batched kernel eliminates that contention—all blocks compete in one pool and get scheduled far more effectively. This could drop wall-clock time from 302ms down to 100-150ms by letting the GPU achieve 35-52% of peak throughput across its 128 SMs. The tradeoff is significant complexity: a custom CUDA kernel with per-AIR descriptors and careful intermediates buffer management.\n\nGiven how risky this is after previous batch attempts failed, I'm reconsidering the approach. Maybe the answer is batching just the SCATTER-mode GKR input eval AIRs on a separate background thread that runs before the main multi-stream work, so it overlaps with the first few GLOBAL launches—but that only saves 3ms, so that's not it either.\n\nLet me look at the Trace Commit phase instead. At APC 300, Trace Commit takes 247ms with poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes running 231ms on the GPU across 25 instances.\n\nI'm wondering if we can overlap the merkle tree compress layers with constraint phase preparation. The compress layers take about 16ms, and during that time we could precompute metadata like interaction layouts and buffer sizes. The constraint phase can't use the commitment hash until the tree root is ready, but we could prepare the CPU-heavy initialization work—building the LogupZerocheckGpu struct, collecting trace interactions, computing buffer sizes—while the compression runs, since that metadata doesn't actually depend on the final commitment hash.\n\nLooking at the constraint phase code, this initialization probably takes 5-10ms, so overlapping it with the 16ms compress layers would save maybe 5-10ms total. That's pretty marginal. I'm starting to think the system is hitting its practical limits with the current architecture—the remaining optimizations are small wins. The most promising path forward would be implementing full batched GKR input evaluation, but that's complex and risky.\n\nGiven the review cycle constraints, I need something concrete and implementable. I'm going to focus on batching the SCATTER-mode GKR input evaluation AIRs into a single descriptor-array kernel launch per worker thread. This approach only targets SCATTER AIRs to avoid intermediates buffer complexity, runs on each thread's stream without cross-stream coordination, and follows the existing batch_mle pattern. It should reduce kernel launch overhead by eliminating around 305 launches and improve GPU utilization for the tiny SCATTER kernels, yielding a modest 10-20ms improvement.\n\nActually, reconsidering whether that improvement justifies the effort—let me think bigger. I'm going to go with batching GKR input evaluation for both GLOBAL and SCATTER modes. The previous batch-round0 failures were due to Rust optimizer issues in a large function and a transient GPU OOM from a clean rebuild, not fundamental problems. Since the GKR input code is modular and in a separate file with clear boundaries, the risk is lower this time. My final plan is to batch SCATTER-mode AIRs using a descriptor-array CUDA kernel, which avoids needing a global intermediates buffer and keeps the scope manageable.\n\nTargeting SCATTER AIRs specifically makes sense because they're the most underutilized—each launches only 4-16 blocks—and consolidating ~19 tiny kernels into one larger batched kernel (~190 blocks) should keep the GPU busier. But I'm realizing a problem: if the batched SCATTER kernel and GLOBAL kernels share the same stream, they serialize, meaning the batched kernel must finish before any GLOBAL kernel can start, which could actually hurt performance.\n\nThe current interleaved approach works well because tiny SCATTER kernels naturally slip into gaps between GLOBAL kernels. Batching per-thread forces all SCATTER work to run as one block before or after GLOBAL work, adding latency rather than hiding it. The real benefit of batching only materializes if I batch across all threads into a single global kernel, letting the GPU scheduler distribute all SCATTER blocks optimally.\n\nA cross-thread batched kernel running on a separate stream concurrent with multi-stream GLOBAL processing could save about 42ms—the SCATTER batch takes ~0.5ms while GLOBAL threads process their remaining work in ~200ms, versus the current ~242ms total. But I need to verify this calculation, since I'm not sure the math fully accounts for all the overhead.\n\nActually, reconsidering the numbers: removing SCATTER AIRs only saves ~3ms per thread due to their small size, so the critical thread would still take ~239ms, and the concurrent SCATTER batch adds another 0.5ms, giving a total of ~239ms—only a 3ms improvement, which isn't worth the added complexity. The real bottleneck is the GLOBAL kernels themselves, which are large and dominate the per-thread execution time, so that's where I need to focus optimization efforts.\n\nThe honest takeaway is that there aren't any easy wins left for GKR input eval—the remaining bottleneck is fundamentally limited by GPU kernel execution speed. Let me shift focus to Trace Commit or WHIR instead. Actually, before I move on, I should check whether there are other inefficiencies in Trace Commit beyond what's already captured in the STARK metrics. Looking at the breakdown, I see Stack traces at ~16ms (already batched), RS code operations around ~65ms, and Merkle tree hashing starting to contribute as well.\n\nThe RS code step involves NTT operations that are difficult to optimize without modifying Plonky3 itself. But I need to figure out whether `batch_expand_pad_wide` is coming from the cuda-backend or Plonky3. Looking at the nsight profiling data, I'm seeing multiple NTT kernel variants with different execution times and instance counts—`batch_ntt_kernel` appears separately from the `_CT_NTT` variants, which suggests they might be coming from different sources.\n\nI'm realizing I can't isolate the trace commit contributions without more detailed profiling, so I'm going to shift focus to the WHIR phase instead. It's taking 99ms and has never been optimized, so there might be some quick wins there. The constraint allows optimizing WHIR since it's part of STARK excluding trace. Let me step back and evaluate my actual options one more time: thread scheduling changes for GKR input eval only gave ~8ms historically and got rejected, batched SCATTER kernels only improve by ~3ms per segment, and full batched GKR is the more comprehensive approach. I need to pick the most defensible optimization and move forward with writing it up.\n\nFull batched GKR kernels would have the highest expected impact at 50-100ms, even though it's complex and risky—it's the natural next step in the optimization trajectory. I'm going to write up the plan for that and present it to the reviewer, knowing we can pivot to alternatives if needed. Actually, wait—what if I approached this differently and changed the number of segments at APC 300 instead? There are currently 2 segments where seg0 has large AIRs and seg1 has small AIRs, but combining them would require changes to the execution trace itself, which isn't something I can control here.\n\nLet me explore a different angle: optimizing the trace commit using multi-stream tree construction. The merkle tree has two phases—row hashing with one large kernel, then log(n) sequential compress layers. Both currently run on the default stream, but the compress layers are inherently sequential since each depends on the previous one, so I can't parallelize them directly. The root hash is only available after all compress layers complete, which blocks the constraint phase. However, if there are multiple commit groups stacked together, I could potentially overlap the compress layers of one group with other work happening in parallel.\n\nLooking at how the coordinator actually calls commit, it seems like there's one tree per commit call, not multiple trees within a single call. The function gets called separately for each commit group like common_main and after_challenge phases, so overlapping opportunities might be limited to between these separate calls rather than within a single stacked commit operation.\n\nGiven that the common_main commit dominates the runtime and the after_challenge commits are relatively small, pipelining between them won't yield much improvement. I'm going to move forward with a concrete optimization proposal that's well-justified and has clear expected gains: batching GKR input evaluation for SCATTER-mode AIRs into a single kernel that runs on a background thread while the main GLOBAL-mode processing continues in parallel.\n\nThe approach separates work items by buffer size during the initial phase, then spawns a background thread to handle all SCATTER AIRs with a batched kernel while 8 worker threads process GLOBAL AIRs concurrently using the existing multi-stream strategy. Once both complete, we sync. The payoff is that each worker thread eliminates roughly 19 sequential SCATTER kernel launches, saving around 3ms per thread on the critical path while the SCATTER batch runs hidden behind the GLOBAL processing—totaling roughly 3-5ms improvement per segment.\n\nThat estimate feels too conservative though. Even if the savings are modest, I should reconsider whether the load balancing improves when fewer AIRs are distributed across threads. Still, 6-10ms total feels below the threshold for a rollback. I'm going to commit to the higher-impact option instead: batch all GKR input evaluation AIRs—both GLOBAL and SCATTER—into descriptor-array kernel launches. It's more complex, but the payoff is worth it. I'll handle the intermediates buffer by pre-computing per-AIR offsets, allocating one large buffer for all intermediates, and passing per-AIR pointers through the descriptor. Let me implement this now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7025,"cache_read_input_tokens":188101,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7025},"output_tokens":29,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4314f39-e91d-47fe-87bd-a8908ed81e1e","uuid":"40200d2e-71a8-4a1c-aacf-bdaaceee3c7c"}
