{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GUecUL9Zt4afCxdxczizNP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PmHCm6MNZ37XE1t2U8JPjK","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0830-batch-scatter-gkr-input-eval/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Batch SCATTER-mode GKR Input Evaluation\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce LogUp GKR input evaluation wall-clock time at APC 300 by batching SCATTER-mode AIR evaluations (buffer_size <= 10) into a single descriptor-array CUDA kernel per segment, launched on a dedicated background thread concurrent with the multi-stream GLOBAL-mode processing.\n\nLogUp GKR is 534ms (40.7% of STARK excl trace at APC 300). The GKR input eval sub-phase is ~302ms. From nsight profiling: 486 GLOBAL-mode instances (464ms GPU time, median 760us) + 305 SCATTER-mode instances (63ms GPU time, median 111us). The SCATTER AIRs each launch a tiny kernel with 4-16 blocks, underutilizing the GPU when launched individually across 8 streams.\n\n**Key mechanism**: By removing ~150 SCATTER AIRs per segment from the 8 multi-stream worker threads, each thread processes ~20 GLOBAL-only AIRs instead of ~39 mixed AIRs. This reduces the critical-thread wall-clock because:\n1. Fewer sequential kernel launches per thread (fewer between-kernel CPU prep gaps)\n2. The SCATTER batch (0.5-1ms, ~1500 blocks) runs on a background thread, completely hidden behind the GLOBAL processing (~200ms)\n3. GLOBAL threads have less total serial work per thread\n\n**Expected improvement**: 15-30ms on GKR input eval at APC 300, based on: each of 8 GLOBAL threads saves ~2-4ms from eliminated SCATTER per-AIR overhead (CPU prep + kernel launch + stream scheduling) × critical path factor.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\n1. **`log_gkr_input_evals()`** (line 215): Entry point.\n   - Line 232-252: Build `Vec<GkrInputWorkItem>`, sort by descending height\n   - Line 254-279: Compute max buffer sizes for pre-allocation\n   - Line 282: Barrier sync\n   - Line 285-289: Set num_threads (8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1)\n   - Line 291-303: Memory budget check\n   - Line 306-321: Pre-allocate per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers`\n   - Line 328-348: Multi-threaded processing: contiguous chunks on height-sorted items\n\n2. **`process_gkr_input_air()`** (line 112): Per-AIR processing.\n   - Line 158-160: `is_global = buffer_size > 10` — the SCATTER/GLOBAL decision\n   - Line 173-187: Calls `logup_gkr_input_eval(is_global, ...)` — single FFI call\n   - Line 189-208: Optional lifting for short traces (height < lifted_height)\n\n3. **CUDA kernel**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`\n   - Line 65-208: `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL>` template\n   - SCATTER mode (GLOBAL=false): uses local `FpExt intermediates[10]` array (line 89-92)\n   - GLOBAL mode (GLOBAL=true): uses global `d_intermediates` buffer (line 85-87)\n   - Line 216-265: Launcher function `_logup_gkr_input_eval`\n\n4. **Rust FFI**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n   - Line 278-292: `_logup_gkr_input_eval` extern declaration\n   - Line 938-968: `logup_gkr_input_eval` safe wrapper\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: New CUDA batched kernel for SCATTER mode\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu` (new code, append to existing file)\n\nDefine a per-AIR descriptor struct and batched kernel:\n\n```cuda\nstruct GkrInputScatterCtx {\n    FracExt *d_fracs;                    // Output: per-AIR region in leaves buffer\n    const Fp *d_preprocessed;            // Per-AIR preprocessed trace\n    const uint64_t *d_main;              // Per-AIR partition pointers\n    const Fp *d_public_values;           // Per-AIR public values\n    const FpExt *d_challenges;           // Shared across all AIRs\n    const Rule *d_rules;                 // Per-AIR interaction DAG rules\n    const size_t *d_used_nodes;          // Per-AIR used node indices\n    const uint32_t *d_pair_idxs;         // Per-AIR pair index mapping\n    size_t used_nodes_len;               // Per-AIR\n    uint32_t permutation_height;         // Per-AIR\n};\n```\n\nThe batched kernel follows the `batch_mle` pattern:\n```cuda\n__global__ void batched_evaluate_interactions_scatter_kernel(\n    const BlockCtx *__restrict__ d_block_ctxs,\n    const GkrInputScatterCtx *__restrict__ d_air_ctxs\n) {\n    BlockCtx block_ctx = d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x];\n    GkrInputScatterCtx ctx = d_air_ctxs[block_ctx.air_idx];\n    \n    uint32_t task_offset = block_ctx.local_block_idx_x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    uint32_t task_stride = ???;  // See grid sizing below\n    \n    FpExt intermediates[10];  // Local array, same as SCATTER mode\n    \n    // Same DAG evaluation loop as existing SCATTER kernel (lines 94-207)\n    // but reading per-AIR data from ctx instead of kernel arguments\n}\n```\n\n**Grid sizing**: Each AIR gets `ceil(height / 256)` blocks. `BlockCtx` maps each block to its AIR index and its local block offset within that AIR. The `task_stride` for each AIR is `num_blocks_for_air * blockDim.x`.\n\n**Launcher function**:\n```cuda\nextern \"C\" int _batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n    const BlockCtx *d_block_ctxs,\n    const GkrInputScatterCtx *d_air_ctxs,\n    uint32_t num_blocks\n) {\n    if (num_blocks == 0) return 0;\n    dim3 grid(num_blocks);\n    dim3 block(256);\n    batched_evaluate_interactions_scatter_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(\n        d_block_ctxs, d_air_ctxs\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### Change 2: Rust FFI binding for batched scatter kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd the `GkrInputScatterCtx` struct (matching C layout) and FFI declaration:\n\n```rust\n#[repr(C)]\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\npub struct GkrInputScatterCtx {\n    pub d_fracs: *mut Frac<EF>,\n    pub d_preprocessed: *const F,\n    pub d_main: *const u64,\n    pub d_public_values: *const F,\n    pub d_challenges: *const EF,\n    pub d_rules: *const std::ffi::c_void,\n    pub d_used_nodes: *const usize,\n    pub d_pair_idxs: *const u32,\n    pub used_nodes_len: usize,\n    pub permutation_height: u32,\n}\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn _batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n        block_ctxs: *const BlockCtx,\n        air_ctxs: *const GkrInputScatterCtx,\n        num_blocks: u32,\n    ) -> i32;\n}\n\npub unsafe fn batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n    block_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,\n    air_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<GkrInputScatterCtx>,\n    num_blocks: u32,\n) -> Result<(), CudaError> { ... }\n```\n\n### Change 3: Restructure `log_gkr_input_evals` to separate SCATTER and GLOBAL AIRs\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nAfter building and sorting work_items (line 252), partition them:\n\n```rust\n// Partition into SCATTER (buffer_size <= 10) and GLOBAL groups\nlet (scatter_items, global_items): (Vec<_>, Vec<_>) = work_items\n    .iter()\n    .enumerate()\n    .partition(|(_, w)| w.pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.buffer_size <= 10);\n```\n\nFor SCATTER AIRs, build descriptor arrays on the main thread (CPU):\n- Build `Vec<GkrInputScatterCtx>` from the SCATTER work items\n- Build `Vec<BlockCtx>` mapping blocks to AIR indices\n- Upload both to device (`DeviceBuffer`)\n\nFor GLOBAL AIRs, continue using the existing multi-stream approach (lines 285-348) but only processing GLOBAL work items.\n\n### Change 4: Launch SCATTER batch on background thread\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nInside the `std::thread::scope` block (or a new scope), spawn a background thread that:\n1. Runs the batched SCATTER kernel via `batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter`\n2. For SCATTER AIRs that need lifting (height != lifted_height), runs `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` + `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat` sequentially\n\nThe background thread runs on its own CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`), concurrent with the 8 GLOBAL worker threads.\n\nThe descriptor arrays and device buffers for the SCATTER batch must be built and uploaded BEFORE spawning the background thread (on the main thread, during Phase 1 preparation).\n\n### Change 5: Handle lifting for batched SCATTER AIRs\n\nFor SCATTER AIRs where `height != lifted_height` (trace height < l_skip power-of-2): the batched kernel writes to a temporary buffer, then per-AIR lifting kernels run. These lifting calls (`frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` + `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat`) are tiny per-AIR and can be launched sequentially on the background thread's stream after the batch kernel completes.\n\nAlternatively, fuse the lifting into the batched kernel by adding `lifted_height` and `norm_factor` to the descriptor. This avoids per-AIR lifting kernel launches but adds complexity to the CUDA kernel. For the prototype, sequential lifting on the background thread is simpler.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Every AIR is processed exactly once. SCATTER AIRs go through the batched kernel; GLOBAL AIRs go through the existing multi-stream path. Both write to the same `leaves` buffer at non-overlapping per-AIR offsets (set during Phase 1 via `leaves_ptr`).\n\n2. **Output equivalence**: The SCATTER batched kernel produces identical results to the per-AIR SCATTER kernel — same DAG evaluation, same output layout. The only difference is launching many AIRs in one kernel vs. one-at-a-time.\n\n3. **Single-thread fallback**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs < 100 threshold), the single-threaded path processes all AIRs sequentially. No SCATTER batching is applied (not worth the descriptor construction overhead for <100 AIRs).\n\n4. **Memory safety**: The `GkrInputScatterCtx` descriptor contains device pointers that are valid for the lifetime of the `log_gkr_input_evals` call. The background thread runs within `std::thread::scope`, guaranteeing it doesn't outlive the data.\n\n5. **APC 0 no-regression**: The >=100 AIR threshold ensures APC 0 uses the unchanged single-threaded path.\n\n6. **GLOBAL path unchanged**: GLOBAL-mode AIRs use the existing multi-stream processing with pre-allocated buffers, unchanged except for receiving fewer work items.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the standard benchmark for APC 300 and APC 000:\n\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <output_path> --recursion\n```\n\nAnalyze with spec.py. Key metrics (APC 300):\n- **LogUp GKR**: current ~534ms, expect 15-30ms improvement\n- **STARK excl trace**: current ~1312ms, expect proportional improvement\n- No regression in Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit, Openings\n\nKey metrics (APC 000):\n- **STARK excl trace**: must not regress (current ~2179ms)\n\nRun each configuration at least twice to account for noise.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of:\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 10ms (median of 2+ runs)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses by more than 20ms\n- Any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 30ms\n- Proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1342,"cache_read_input_tokens":210494,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1342},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4314f39-e91d-47fe-87bd-a8908ed81e1e","uuid":"91d55694-eb8d-4684-b01c-687d65d50760"}
