{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0127QM7rw2jeMAiyQbGHPMBh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Increase GKR Input Eval Streams + Round-Robin Distribution\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce LogUp GKR input evaluation wall-clock time at APC 300 by increasing `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS` from 8 to 16 and switching from contiguous chunk distribution to interleaved (round-robin) work assignment.\n6\t\n7\tLogUp GKR is the dominant bottleneck at 534ms (40.7% of STARK excl trace). The GKR input eval sub-phase accounts for ~302ms of this across 2 segments (~242ms seg0, ~62ms seg1). The current implementation uses 8 OS threads with contiguous chunking on height-sorted work items, which gives thread 0 all the largest AIRs — worst-case load balance.\n8\t\n9\tThe previous attempt to apply round-robin alone (task `gkr-input-round-robin`) saved only 8ms and was reverted. However, that attempt used 8 threads. The combination of 16 threads (finer work granularity, each thread gets ~20 AIRs instead of ~39) with round-robin distribution has not been tried. The pre-allocated per-thread buffers (from `prealloc-gkr-input-buffers`) eliminate the mutex contention that previously limited thread-count scaling.\n10\t\n11\t## Current Code Path\n12\t\n13\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n14\t\n15\t1. **Constant** (line 30): `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8`\n16\t\n17\t2. **`log_gkr_input_evals()`** (line 215):\n18\t   - Line 232-252: Build work items, sort by descending height\n19\t   - Line 254-279: Compute max buffer sizes across all AIRs\n20\t   - Line 282: Barrier sync (`current_stream_sync`)\n21\t   - Line 285-289: Set `num_threads` = 8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1\n22\t   - Line 291-303: Memory budget check — halve `num_threads` while `num_threads * per_thread_bytes > 2GB`\n23\t   - Line 306-321: Pre-allocate per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers` (intermediates, public_values, partition_ptrs, tmp)\n24\t   - Line 323-327: Single-thread fallback path (num_threads <= 1)\n25\t   - **Line 329**: `let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);` — contiguous chunking\n26\t   - **Line 330-348**: `std::thread::scope` — threads iterate over contiguous `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)`\n27\t\n28\t3. **`process_gkr_input_air()`** (line 112):\n29\t   - Per-AIR: collect partition pointers, H2D copy public_values + partition_ptrs, launch `logup_gkr_input_eval` kernel, optional lifting for short traces\n30\t   - Uses pre-allocated thread buffers (no allocation per AIR)\n31\t\n32\t**Why it's slow**: Contiguous chunking on height-sorted items means thread 0 gets the 39 largest AIRs (including several with 5-13ms kernel times). Other threads finish much earlier. The wall-clock is determined by thread 0's total serialized kernel time.\n33\t\n34\t## Changes\n35\t\n36\t### Change 1: Increase thread count constant\n37\t\n38\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, line 30\n39\t\n40\t```rust\n41\t// Before:\n42\tconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8;\n43\t\n44\t// After:\n45\tconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 16;\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\t**Why**: More threads provide finer-grained work distribution across the 312 AIRs in APC 300 seg0 (~20 AIRs/thread instead of ~39). Each thread gets fewer large AIRs, reducing the critical-path thread time. Pre-allocated buffers make this viable without mutex contention.\n49\t\n50\t**Memory impact**: At ~100MB per thread buffer set, 16 threads use ~1.6GB vs ~800MB for 8 threads. The memory budget check (line 296) will automatically halve to 8 if per-thread bytes × 16 > 2GB, so this self-corrects if the max buffer size is unusually large.\n51\t\n52\t### Change 2: Switch from contiguous chunks to round-robin distribution\n53\t\n54\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, lines 329-348\n55\t\n56\tReplace the contiguous `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)` dispatch with interleaved (round-robin) index assignment, following the same pattern used in Round 0 (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1280-1291).\n57\t\n58\t```rust\n59\t// Before (lines 329-348):\n60\tlet chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\n61\tstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n62\t    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n63\t        .into_iter()\n64\t        .zip(work_items.chunks(chunk_size))\n65\t        .map(|(mut bufs, chunk)| {\n66\t            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n67\t                for w in chunk {\n68\t                    process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?;\n69\t                }\n70\t                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n71\t                Ok(())\n72\t            })\n73\t        })\n74\t        .collect();\n75\t    for handle in handles {\n76\t        handle.join().unwrap()?;\n77\t    }\n78\t    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n79\t})?;\n80\t\n81\t// After:\n82\t// Interleaved (round-robin) assignment on the height-sorted list.\n83\t// Work items are already sorted by descending height. Round-robin\n84\t// distributes them so each thread gets a balanced mix of large and\n85\t// small AIRs (thread 0 gets items 0, N, 2N, ...; thread 1 gets\n86\t// items 1, N+1, 2N+1, ...).\n87\tlet mut thread_indices: Vec<Vec<usize>> =\n88\t    (0..num_threads).map(|_| Vec::new()).collect();\n89\tfor (idx, _) in work_items.iter().enumerate() {\n90\t    thread_indices[idx % num_threads].push(idx);\n91\t}\n92\t\n93\tstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n94\t    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n95\t        .into_iter()\n96\t        .zip(thread_indices.into_iter())\n97\t        .map(|(mut bufs, indices)| {\n98\t            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n99\t                for &idx in &indices {\n100\t                    process_gkr_input_air(&work_items[idx], &mut bufs)?;\n101\t                }\n102\t                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n103\t                Ok(())\n104\t            })\n105\t        })\n106\t        .collect();\n107\t    for handle in handles {\n108\t        handle.join().unwrap()?;\n109\t    }\n110\t    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n111\t})?;\n112\t```\n113\t\n114\t**Why**: Round-robin on height-sorted items ensures each thread gets every Nth AIR by descending height. Thread 0 gets the 1st, 17th, 33rd, ... largest AIRs (with 16 threads). This distributes both large and small AIRs evenly, balancing GPU kernel time and per-AIR fixed overhead across threads.\n115\t\n116\t**Borrow checker note**: The `work_items` slice must be accessible from all threads. Since `thread::scope` guarantees the spawned threads don't outlive the scope, `&work_items[idx]` borrows are safe. The `thread_indices` Vec is moved into the iterator, and each thread gets ownership of its `Vec<usize>`.\n117\t\n118\t## Invariants\n119\t\n120\t1. **Correctness**: Every work item is processed exactly once. Round-robin assignment is a partition of indices `0..work_items.len()` across `num_threads` threads. No work item is skipped or duplicated.\n121\t\n122\t2. **Output equivalence**: Each thread writes to its work items' pre-computed `leaves_ptr` offsets (set during Phase 1). These offsets are per-AIR and non-overlapping. The output `leaves` buffer is identical regardless of thread assignment order.\n123\t\n124\t3. **Single-thread fallback**: The `num_threads <= 1` path (line 323-327) is unchanged. AIR count < 100 still uses single-threaded sequential processing.\n125\t\n126\t4. **Memory budget**: The existing budget check (line 296) automatically halves `num_threads` if total pre-allocation exceeds 2GB. With 16 threads at ~100MB each = 1.6GB, this check passes. If a future workload has larger buffers, it degrades gracefully to 8 or fewer threads.\n127\t\n128\t5. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs < 100 threshold), the single-threaded path is taken. No change in behavior.\n129\t\n130\t## Measurement Plan\n131\t\n132\tRun the standard benchmark for APC 300 and APC 000:\n133\t\n134\t```bash\n135\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n136\t/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <output_path> --recursion\n137\t```\n138\t\n139\tAnalyze with spec.py:\n140\t```bash\n141\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\t**Key metrics to compare (APC 300)**:\n145\t- LogUp GKR: baseline 534ms, current ~534ms, expect improvement\n146\t- STARK excl trace: baseline 2455ms, current ~1312ms, expect improvement\n147\t- No regression in other phases (Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit, Openings)\n148\t\n149\t**Key metrics to compare (APC 000)**:\n150\t- STARK excl trace: must not regress (current ~2179ms)\n151\t- The single-threaded fallback ensures no change at APC 0\n152\t\n153\t**Per-segment GKR input eval timing**: Check the `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals` tracing span in the benchmark output for per-segment breakdown.\n154\t\n155\tRun each configuration at least twice to account for measurement noise.\n156\t\n157\t## Rollback Criteria\n158\t\n159\tRevert if ANY of these conditions hold:\n160\t- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 15ms (median of 2+ runs)\n161\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses by more than 20ms\n162\t- Any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 30ms\n163\t- Any correctness failure (proof verification fails)\n164\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4314f39-e91d-47fe-87bd-a8908ed81e1e","uuid":"45a8ff87-ad6c-4be6-8dc2-c8ce39a7543b","timestamp":"2026-04-14T09:13:59.430Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0830-batch-scatter-gkr-input-eval/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Increase GKR Input Eval Streams + Round-Robin Distribution\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce LogUp GKR input evaluation wall-clock time at APC 300 by increasing `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS` from 8 to 16 and switching from contiguous chunk distribution to interleaved (round-robin) work assignment.\n\nLogUp GKR is the dominant bottleneck at 534ms (40.7% of STARK excl trace). The GKR input eval sub-phase accounts for ~302ms of this across 2 segments (~242ms seg0, ~62ms seg1). The current implementation uses 8 OS threads with contiguous chunking on height-sorted work items, which gives thread 0 all the largest AIRs — worst-case load balance.\n\nThe previous attempt to apply round-robin alone (task `gkr-input-round-robin`) saved only 8ms and was reverted. However, that attempt used 8 threads. The combination of 16 threads (finer work granularity, each thread gets ~20 AIRs instead of ~39) with round-robin distribution has not been tried. The pre-allocated per-thread buffers (from `prealloc-gkr-input-buffers`) eliminate the mutex contention that previously limited thread-count scaling.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\n1. **Constant** (line 30): `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8`\n\n2. **`log_gkr_input_evals()`** (line 215):\n   - Line 232-252: Build work items, sort by descending height\n   - Line 254-279: Compute max buffer sizes across all AIRs\n   - Line 282: Barrier sync (`current_stream_sync`)\n   - Line 285-289: Set `num_threads` = 8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1\n   - Line 291-303: Memory budget check — halve `num_threads` while `num_threads * per_thread_bytes > 2GB`\n   - Line 306-321: Pre-allocate per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers` (intermediates, public_values, partition_ptrs, tmp)\n   - Line 323-327: Single-thread fallback path (num_threads <= 1)\n   - **Line 329**: `let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);` — contiguous chunking\n   - **Line 330-348**: `std::thread::scope` — threads iterate over contiguous `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)`\n\n3. **`process_gkr_input_air()`** (line 112):\n   - Per-AIR: collect partition pointers, H2D copy public_values + partition_ptrs, launch `logup_gkr_input_eval` kernel, optional lifting for short traces\n   - Uses pre-allocated thread buffers (no allocation per AIR)\n\n**Why it's slow**: Contiguous chunking on height-sorted items means thread 0 gets the 39 largest AIRs (including several with 5-13ms kernel times). Other threads finish much earlier. The wall-clock is determined by thread 0's total serialized kernel time.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Increase thread count constant\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, line 30\n\n```rust\n// Before:\nconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8;\n\n// After:\nconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 16;\n```\n\n**Why**: More threads provide finer-grained work distribution across the 312 AIRs in APC 300 seg0 (~20 AIRs/thread instead of ~39). Each thread gets fewer large AIRs, reducing the critical-path thread time. Pre-allocated buffers make this viable without mutex contention.\n\n**Memory impact**: At ~100MB per thread buffer set, 16 threads use ~1.6GB vs ~800MB for 8 threads. The memory budget check (line 296) will automatically halve to 8 if per-thread bytes × 16 > 2GB, so this self-corrects if the max buffer size is unusually large.\n\n### Change 2: Switch from contiguous chunks to round-robin distribution\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, lines 329-348\n\nReplace the contiguous `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)` dispatch with interleaved (round-robin) index assignment, following the same pattern used in Round 0 (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1280-1291).\n\n```rust\n// Before (lines 329-348):\nlet chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n        .into_iter()\n        .zip(work_items.chunks(chunk_size))\n        .map(|(mut bufs, chunk)| {\n            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n                for w in chunk {\n                    process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?;\n                }\n                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n                Ok(())\n            })\n        })\n        .collect();\n    for handle in handles {\n        handle.join().unwrap()?;\n    }\n    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n})?;\n\n// After:\n// Interleaved (round-robin) assignment on the height-sorted list.\n// Work items are already sorted by descending height. Round-robin\n// distributes them so each thread gets a balanced mix of large and\n// small AIRs (thread 0 gets items 0, N, 2N, ...; thread 1 gets\n// items 1, N+1, 2N+1, ...).\nlet mut thread_indices: Vec<Vec<usize>> =\n    (0..num_threads).map(|_| Vec::new()).collect();\nfor (idx, _) in work_items.iter().enumerate() {\n    thread_indices[idx % num_threads].push(idx);\n}\n\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n        .into_iter()\n        .zip(thread_indices.into_iter())\n        .map(|(mut bufs, indices)| {\n            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n                for &idx in &indices {\n                    process_gkr_input_air(&work_items[idx], &mut bufs)?;\n                }\n                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n                Ok(())\n            })\n        })\n        .collect();\n    for handle in handles {\n        handle.join().unwrap()?;\n    }\n    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n})?;\n```\n\n**Why**: Round-robin on height-sorted items ensures each thread gets every Nth AIR by descending height. Thread 0 gets the 1st, 17th, 33rd, ... largest AIRs (with 16 threads). This distributes both large and small AIRs evenly, balancing GPU kernel time and per-AIR fixed overhead across threads.\n\n**Borrow checker note**: The `work_items` slice must be accessible from all threads. Since `thread::scope` guarantees the spawned threads don't outlive the scope, `&work_items[idx]` borrows are safe. The `thread_indices` Vec is moved into the iterator, and each thread gets ownership of its `Vec<usize>`.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Every work item is processed exactly once. Round-robin assignment is a partition of indices `0..work_items.len()` across `num_threads` threads. No work item is skipped or duplicated.\n\n2. **Output equivalence**: Each thread writes to its work items' pre-computed `leaves_ptr` offsets (set during Phase 1). These offsets are per-AIR and non-overlapping. The output `leaves` buffer is identical regardless of thread assignment order.\n\n3. **Single-thread fallback**: The `num_threads <= 1` path (line 323-327) is unchanged. AIR count < 100 still uses single-threaded sequential processing.\n\n4. **Memory budget**: The existing budget check (line 296) automatically halves `num_threads` if total pre-allocation exceeds 2GB. With 16 threads at ~100MB each = 1.6GB, this check passes. If a future workload has larger buffers, it degrades gracefully to 8 or fewer threads.\n\n5. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs < 100 threshold), the single-threaded path is taken. No change in behavior.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the standard benchmark for APC 300 and APC 000:\n\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <output_path> --recursion\n```\n\nAnalyze with spec.py:\n```bash\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>\n```\n\n**Key metrics to compare (APC 300)**:\n- LogUp GKR: baseline 534ms, current ~534ms, expect improvement\n- STARK excl trace: baseline 2455ms, current ~1312ms, expect improvement\n- No regression in other phases (Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit, Openings)\n\n**Key metrics to compare (APC 000)**:\n- STARK excl trace: must not regress (current ~2179ms)\n- The single-threaded fallback ensures no change at APC 0\n\n**Per-segment GKR input eval timing**: Check the `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals` tracing span in the benchmark output for per-segment breakdown.\n\nRun each configuration at least twice to account for measurement noise.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of these conditions hold:\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 15ms (median of 2+ runs)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses by more than 20ms\n- Any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 30ms\n- Any correctness failure (proof verification fails)\n","numLines":164,"startLine":1,"totalLines":164}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YZNic7dCTgfLMuWYUFxifp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014oiL618WFDf8jUeq8RvNUM","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0830-batch-scatter-gkr-input-eval/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Batch SCATTER-mode GKR Input Evaluation\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce LogUp GKR input evaluation wall-clock time at APC 300 by batching SCATTER-mode AIR evaluations (buffer_size <= 10) into a single descriptor-array CUDA kernel per segment, launched on a dedicated background thread concurrent with the existing multi-stream GLOBAL-mode processing.\n\nLogUp GKR is 534ms (40.7% of STARK excl trace at APC 300). The GKR input eval sub-phase is ~302ms across 2 segments (~242ms seg0, ~62ms seg1). From nsight profiling: 486 GLOBAL-mode instances (464ms GPU time, median 760us) + 305 SCATTER-mode instances (63ms GPU time, median 111us). SCATTER AIRs each launch a tiny kernel with 4-16 blocks that underutilizes the 128 SMs.\n\n**Key mechanism**: Remove ~150 SCATTER AIRs per segment from the 8 multi-stream worker threads. Each thread processes ~20 GLOBAL-only AIRs instead of ~39 mixed AIRs. The SCATTER batch runs on a 9th background thread, hidden behind the GLOBAL processing. Each GLOBAL worker thread saves ~19 per-AIR overheads: CPU preparation (~25us), kernel launch (~10us), and between-kernel scheduling gap (~50us). Per-thread savings: ~1.6ms. The critical-path thread improves because its total serial work is shorter.\n\nAdditionally, the batched SCATTER kernel (one launch, ~1500 blocks) processes all SCATTER AIRs with optimal GPU block scheduling, vs the current approach where tiny 4-16 block kernels leave most SMs idle during each launch.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\n1. **`log_gkr_input_evals()`** (line 215): Entry point.\n   - Line 232-252: Build `Vec<GkrInputWorkItem>`, sort by descending height\n   - Line 254-279: Compute max buffer sizes for pre-allocation\n   - Line 282: Barrier sync\n   - Line 285-289: Set num_threads (8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1)\n   - Line 291-303: Memory budget check (halve num_threads if pre-alloc > 2GB)\n   - Line 306-321: Pre-allocate per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers`\n   - Line 328-348: Multi-threaded processing: contiguous chunks, all AIR types mixed\n\n2. **`process_gkr_input_air()`** (line 112): Per-AIR processing.\n   - Line 125-129: Build partition pointers from cached_mains + common_main\n   - Line 141-148: Upload public_values to device via `copy_to`\n   - Line 152-156: Upload partition_ptrs to device via `copy_to`\n   - Line 158-160: `is_global = buffer_size > 10` — determines SCATTER vs GLOBAL mode\n   - Line 173-187: Single FFI call: `logup_gkr_input_eval(is_global, ...)`\n   - Line 189-208: Conditional lifting: if height != lifted_height, scale + repeat\n\n3. **CUDA kernel**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`\n   - Line 65-208: `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL>` template\n   - SCATTER (GLOBAL=false, line 89-92): `FpExt intermediates[10]` — local array, max 10 nodes\n   - GLOBAL (GLOBAL=true, line 85-87): `d_intermediates` global buffer, stride = task_stride\n   - Line 17-63: `evaluate_dag_entry_gkr()` — switch on SourceInfo.type to load data\n   - Line 94-207: Per-row loop: iterate d_used_nodes, evaluate DAG, write to d_fracs\n   - Line 216-265: Launcher: `grid = kernel_launch_params(is_global ? TASK_SIZE : height, 256)`\n\n4. **FFI**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n   - Line 278-292: extern declaration\n   - Line 938-968: safe wrapper `logup_gkr_input_eval()`\n\n**Existing batched pattern reference**: `ZerocheckCtx` (line 48-58 of `cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`) + `BlockCtx` (line 17-22) used by `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel` in `cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`. Each block reads `BlockCtx` to find its AIR, then loads the per-AIR `ZerocheckCtx` struct containing `d_rules`, `d_used_nodes`, `used_nodes_len`, `buffer_size`, etc.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Define GkrInputScatterCtx descriptor struct\n\n**Files**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu` (CUDA) and `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (Rust)\n\nCUDA side (add to gkr_input.cu, before the launcher function):\n```cuda\nstruct GkrInputScatterCtx {\n    FracExt *d_fracs;               // Per-AIR output pointer (into leaves buffer)\n    const Fp *d_preprocessed;       // Per-AIR preprocessed trace (or null)\n    const uint64_t *d_main;         // Per-AIR partition pointers (device array)\n    const Fp *d_public_values;      // Per-AIR public values (or null)\n    const FpExt *d_challenges;      // Shared challenge vector (same for all AIRs)\n    const Rule *d_rules;            // Per-AIR interaction DAG rules\n    const size_t *d_used_nodes;     // Per-AIR used node indices\n    const uint32_t *d_pair_idxs;    // Per-AIR pair index mapping\n    size_t used_nodes_len;          // Per-AIR\n    uint32_t permutation_height;    // Per-AIR trace height\n    uint32_t num_blocks;            // Number of blocks assigned to this AIR\n};\n```\n\nRust side (add to `cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, near existing context structs):\n```rust\n#[repr(C)]\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\npub struct GkrInputScatterCtx {\n    pub d_fracs: *mut Frac<EF>,\n    pub d_preprocessed: *const F,\n    pub d_main: *const u64,\n    pub d_public_values: *const F,\n    pub d_challenges: *const EF,\n    pub d_rules: *const std::ffi::c_void,\n    pub d_used_nodes: *const usize,\n    pub d_pair_idxs: *const u32,\n    pub used_nodes_len: usize,\n    pub permutation_height: u32,\n    pub num_blocks: u32,\n}\nunsafe impl Send for GkrInputScatterCtx {}\nunsafe impl Sync for GkrInputScatterCtx {}\n```\n\n### Change 2: New batched SCATTER kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu` (append)\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void batched_evaluate_interactions_scatter_kernel(\n    const BlockCtx *__restrict__ d_block_ctxs,\n    const GkrInputScatterCtx *__restrict__ d_air_ctxs\n) {\n    BlockCtx block_ctx = d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x];\n    GkrInputScatterCtx ctx = d_air_ctxs[block_ctx.air_idx];\n\n    // Per-block task distribution within this AIR\n    uint32_t local_block = block_ctx.local_block_idx_x;\n    uint32_t task_offset = local_block * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    uint32_t task_stride = ctx.num_blocks * blockDim.x;\n\n    FpExt intermediates[10];  // Local array, same as SCATTER mode\n    uint32_t intermediate_stride = 1;\n\n    // Same DAG evaluation loop as existing SCATTER kernel (gkr_input.cu lines 94-207)\n    // using ctx.d_rules, ctx.d_used_nodes, ctx.d_pair_idxs, etc.\n    // Guard: if (task_offset >= ctx.permutation_height) return;\n    // ... (copy evaluation loop body from lines 94-207, replacing\n    //      kernel arguments with ctx.field references)\n}\n```\n\nLauncher:\n```cuda\nextern \"C\" int _batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n    const BlockCtx *d_block_ctxs,\n    const GkrInputScatterCtx *d_air_ctxs,\n    uint32_t num_blocks\n) {\n    if (num_blocks == 0) return 0;\n    dim3 grid(num_blocks);\n    dim3 block(256);\n    batched_evaluate_interactions_scatter_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(\n        d_block_ctxs, d_air_ctxs\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### Change 3: Rust FFI + safe wrapper for batched scatter kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd extern declaration and safe wrapper:\n```rust\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn _batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n        block_ctxs: *const BlockCtx,\n        air_ctxs: *const GkrInputScatterCtx,\n        num_blocks: u32,\n    ) -> i32;\n}\n\npub unsafe fn batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n    block_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,\n    air_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<GkrInputScatterCtx>,\n    num_blocks: u32,\n) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n    check_error(_batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n        block_ctxs.as_ptr(),\n        air_ctxs.as_ptr(),\n        num_blocks,\n    ))\n}\n```\n\n### Change 4: Restructure `log_gkr_input_evals` to separate SCATTER and GLOBAL AIRs\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nAfter building and sorting work_items (line 252), partition by mode:\n\n```rust\n// Separate SCATTER and GLOBAL work items (preserving height-sorted order within each group)\nlet mut scatter_indices: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\nlet mut global_indices: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\nfor (idx, w) in work_items.iter().enumerate() {\n    if w.pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.buffer_size <= 10 {\n        scatter_indices.push(idx);\n    } else {\n        global_indices.push(idx);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBuild SCATTER descriptor arrays (main thread, before spawning workers):\n```rust\n// Build BlockCtx and GkrInputScatterCtx for all SCATTER AIRs\nlet mut block_ctxs: Vec<BlockCtx> = Vec::new();\nlet mut air_ctxs: Vec<GkrInputScatterCtx> = Vec::new();\nfor (air_local_idx, &work_idx) in scatter_indices.iter().enumerate() {\n    let w = &work_items[work_idx];\n    let height = w.air_ctx.height() as u32;\n    let num_air_blocks = height.div_ceil(256);\n\n    for local_block in 0..num_air_blocks {\n        block_ctxs.push(BlockCtx {\n            local_block_idx_x: local_block,\n            air_idx: air_local_idx as u32,\n        });\n    }\n\n    // Build per-AIR context (collecting pointers from existing data structures)\n    air_ctxs.push(GkrInputScatterCtx { /* fields from work item */ });\n}\nlet d_block_ctxs = block_ctxs.to_device()?;\nlet d_air_ctxs = air_ctxs.to_device()?;\nlet total_scatter_blocks = block_ctxs.len() as u32;\n```\n\nModify the multi-threaded section to only process GLOBAL AIRs:\n```rust\n// Adjust num_threads based on GLOBAL count (not total)\nlet mut num_threads = if global_indices.len() >= 100 {\n    NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS.min(global_indices.len())\n} else if global_indices.is_empty() {\n    0\n} else {\n    1\n};\n// ... memory budget check on GLOBAL-only buffer sizes ...\n```\n\n### Change 5: Launch SCATTER batch on background thread\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nInside the `std::thread::scope` block, add a background thread for SCATTER batch:\n\n```rust\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    // Background thread: batched SCATTER evaluation\n    let scatter_handle = if total_scatter_blocks > 0 {\n        Some(s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n            unsafe {\n                batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n                    &d_block_ctxs, &d_air_ctxs, total_scatter_blocks\n                )?;\n            }\n            // Lifting for SCATTER AIRs that need it\n            for &work_idx in &scatter_indices {\n                let w = &work_items[work_idx];\n                let height = w.air_ctx.height();\n                let lifted_height = max(height, 1 << w.l_skip);\n                if height != lifted_height {\n                    // Launch per-AIR lifting kernels on this thread's stream\n                    // (sequential, after batch kernel completes)\n                    // ... frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp + frac_matrix_vertically_repeat\n                }\n            }\n            current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n            Ok(())\n        }))\n    } else {\n        None\n    };\n\n    // Worker threads: GLOBAL-mode AIRs only\n    let global_handles: Vec<_> = /* ... existing multi-stream dispatch using global_indices ... */;\n\n    // Join all\n    if let Some(h) = scatter_handle { h.join().unwrap()?; }\n    for h in global_handles { h.join().unwrap()?; }\n    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n})?;\n```\n\n**Borrow checker**: `work_items`, `scatter_indices`, `d_block_ctxs`, `d_air_ctxs` are all borrowed/captured by the scatter thread. Since they're within `thread::scope`, the borrows are safe. `d_block_ctxs` and `d_air_ctxs` are `DeviceBuffer` values that are moved into the scatter thread closure.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Every AIR is processed exactly once. SCATTER AIRs go through the batched kernel; GLOBAL AIRs go through the existing multi-stream path. Both write to the same `leaves` buffer at pre-computed non-overlapping per-AIR offsets.\n\n2. **Output equivalence**: The batched SCATTER kernel uses the same DAG evaluation logic as the existing `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<false>`, just with per-block descriptors instead of per-launch arguments.\n\n3. **Single-thread fallback**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs < 100 threshold), all AIRs are processed sequentially on the main thread (unchanged).\n\n4. **Memory safety**: `GkrInputScatterCtx` pointers reference device data that is valid for the lifetime of `log_gkr_input_evals`. No SCATTER intermediates buffer is needed (local array of 10 elements).\n\n5. **APC 0 no-regression**: Threshold check ensures single-threaded path is used at APC 0.\n\n6. **GLOBAL path unchanged**: GLOBAL-mode AIRs use the existing multi-stream processing with pre-allocated per-thread buffers.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the standard benchmark:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path> --recursion\n```\n\nKey metrics (APC 300):\n- **LogUp GKR**: current ~534ms, expect 10-25ms improvement\n- **STARK excl trace**: current ~1312ms, expect proportional improvement\n- No regression in Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit, Openings\n\nKey metrics (APC 000): must not regress.\n\nRun each configuration at least twice.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of:\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 10ms (median of 2+ runs)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses by more than 20ms\n- Any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 30ms\n- Proof verification fails\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3184,"cache_read_input_tokens":215415,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3184},"output_tokens":69,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4314f39-e91d-47fe-87bd-a8908ed81e1e","uuid":"a5a141c9-8fbb-4bf7-97fc-0e0af17dafaa"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014oiL618WFDf8jUeq8RvNUM","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0830-batch-scatter-gkr-input-eval/plan.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4314f39-e91d-47fe-87bd-a8908ed81e1e","uuid":"5d504a99-a2a3-4101-ae18-5a776374992a","timestamp":"2026-04-14T09:15:16.758Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0830-batch-scatter-gkr-input-eval/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Batch SCATTER-mode GKR Input Evaluation\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce LogUp GKR input evaluation wall-clock time at APC 300 by batching SCATTER-mode AIR evaluations (buffer_size <= 10) into a single descriptor-array CUDA kernel per segment, launched on a dedicated background thread concurrent with the existing multi-stream GLOBAL-mode processing.\n\nLogUp GKR is 534ms (40.7% of STARK excl trace at APC 300). The GKR input eval sub-phase is ~302ms across 2 segments (~242ms seg0, ~62ms seg1). From nsight profiling: 486 GLOBAL-mode instances (464ms GPU time, median 760us) + 305 SCATTER-mode instances (63ms GPU time, median 111us). SCATTER AIRs each launch a tiny kernel with 4-16 blocks that underutilizes the 128 SMs.\n\n**Key mechanism**: Remove ~150 SCATTER AIRs per segment from the 8 multi-stream worker threads. Each thread processes ~20 GLOBAL-only AIRs instead of ~39 mixed AIRs. The SCATTER batch runs on a 9th background thread, hidden behind the GLOBAL processing. Each GLOBAL worker thread saves ~19 per-AIR overheads: CPU preparation (~25us), kernel launch (~10us), and between-kernel scheduling gap (~50us). Per-thread savings: ~1.6ms. The critical-path thread improves because its total serial work is shorter.\n\nAdditionally, the batched SCATTER kernel (one launch, ~1500 blocks) processes all SCATTER AIRs with optimal GPU block scheduling, vs the current approach where tiny 4-16 block kernels leave most SMs idle during each launch.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\n1. **`log_gkr_input_evals()`** (line 215): Entry point.\n   - Line 232-252: Build `Vec<GkrInputWorkItem>`, sort by descending height\n   - Line 254-279: Compute max buffer sizes for pre-allocation\n   - Line 282: Barrier sync\n   - Line 285-289: Set num_threads (8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1)\n   - Line 291-303: Memory budget check (halve num_threads if pre-alloc > 2GB)\n   - Line 306-321: Pre-allocate per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers`\n   - Line 328-348: Multi-threaded processing: contiguous chunks, all AIR types mixed\n\n2. **`process_gkr_input_air()`** (line 112): Per-AIR processing.\n   - Line 125-129: Build partition pointers from cached_mains + common_main\n   - Line 141-148: Upload public_values to device via `copy_to`\n   - Line 152-156: Upload partition_ptrs to device via `copy_to`\n   - Line 158-160: `is_global = buffer_size > 10` — determines SCATTER vs GLOBAL mode\n   - Line 173-187: Single FFI call: `logup_gkr_input_eval(is_global, ...)`\n   - Line 189-208: Conditional lifting: if height != lifted_height, scale + repeat\n\n3. **CUDA kernel**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`\n   - Line 65-208: `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL>` template\n   - SCATTER (GLOBAL=false, line 89-92): `FpExt intermediates[10]` — local array, max 10 nodes\n   - GLOBAL (GLOBAL=true, line 85-87): `d_intermediates` global buffer, stride = task_stride\n   - Line 17-63: `evaluate_dag_entry_gkr()` — switch on SourceInfo.type to load data\n   - Line 94-207: Per-row loop: iterate d_used_nodes, evaluate DAG, write to d_fracs\n   - Line 216-265: Launcher: `grid = kernel_launch_params(is_global ? TASK_SIZE : height, 256)`\n\n4. **FFI**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n   - Line 278-292: extern declaration\n   - Line 938-968: safe wrapper `logup_gkr_input_eval()`\n\n**Existing batched pattern reference**: `ZerocheckCtx` (line 48-58 of `cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`) + `BlockCtx` (line 17-22) used by `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel` in `cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`. Each block reads `BlockCtx` to find its AIR, then loads the per-AIR `ZerocheckCtx` struct containing `d_rules`, `d_used_nodes`, `used_nodes_len`, `buffer_size`, etc.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Define GkrInputScatterCtx descriptor struct\n\n**Files**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu` (CUDA) and `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (Rust)\n\nCUDA side (add to gkr_input.cu, before the launcher function):\n```cuda\nstruct GkrInputScatterCtx {\n    FracExt *d_fracs;               // Per-AIR output pointer (into leaves buffer)\n    const Fp *d_preprocessed;       // Per-AIR preprocessed trace (or null)\n    const uint64_t *d_main;         // Per-AIR partition pointers (device array)\n    const Fp *d_public_values;      // Per-AIR public values (or null)\n    const FpExt *d_challenges;      // Shared challenge vector (same for all AIRs)\n    const Rule *d_rules;            // Per-AIR interaction DAG rules\n    const size_t *d_used_nodes;     // Per-AIR used node indices\n    const uint32_t *d_pair_idxs;    // Per-AIR pair index mapping\n    size_t used_nodes_len;          // Per-AIR\n    uint32_t permutation_height;    // Per-AIR trace height\n    uint32_t num_blocks;            // Number of blocks assigned to this AIR\n};\n```\n\nRust side (add to `cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, near existing context structs):\n```rust\n#[repr(C)]\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\npub struct GkrInputScatterCtx {\n    pub d_fracs: *mut Frac<EF>,\n    pub d_preprocessed: *const F,\n    pub d_main: *const u64,\n    pub d_public_values: *const F,\n    pub d_challenges: *const EF,\n    pub d_rules: *const std::ffi::c_void,\n    pub d_used_nodes: *const usize,\n    pub d_pair_idxs: *const u32,\n    pub used_nodes_len: usize,\n    pub permutation_height: u32,\n    pub num_blocks: u32,\n}\nunsafe impl Send for GkrInputScatterCtx {}\nunsafe impl Sync for GkrInputScatterCtx {}\n```\n\n### Change 2: New batched SCATTER kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu` (append)\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void batched_evaluate_interactions_scatter_kernel(\n    const BlockCtx *__restrict__ d_block_ctxs,\n    const GkrInputScatterCtx *__restrict__ d_air_ctxs\n) {\n    BlockCtx block_ctx = d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x];\n    GkrInputScatterCtx ctx = d_air_ctxs[block_ctx.air_idx];\n\n    // Per-block task distribution within this AIR\n    uint32_t local_block = block_ctx.local_block_idx_x;\n    uint32_t task_offset = local_block * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    uint32_t task_stride = ctx.num_blocks * blockDim.x;\n\n    FpExt intermediates[10];  // Local array, same as SCATTER mode\n    uint32_t intermediate_stride = 1;\n\n    // Same DAG evaluation loop as existing SCATTER kernel (gkr_input.cu lines 94-207)\n    // using ctx.d_rules, ctx.d_used_nodes, ctx.d_pair_idxs, etc.\n    // Guard: if (task_offset >= ctx.permutation_height) return;\n    // ... (copy evaluation loop body from lines 94-207, replacing\n    //      kernel arguments with ctx.field references)\n}\n```\n\nLauncher:\n```cuda\nextern \"C\" int _batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n    const BlockCtx *d_block_ctxs,\n    const GkrInputScatterCtx *d_air_ctxs,\n    uint32_t num_blocks\n) {\n    if (num_blocks == 0) return 0;\n    dim3 grid(num_blocks);\n    dim3 block(256);\n    batched_evaluate_interactions_scatter_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(\n        d_block_ctxs, d_air_ctxs\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### Change 3: Rust FFI + safe wrapper for batched scatter kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd extern declaration and safe wrapper:\n```rust\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn _batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n        block_ctxs: *const BlockCtx,\n        air_ctxs: *const GkrInputScatterCtx,\n        num_blocks: u32,\n    ) -> i32;\n}\n\npub unsafe fn batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n    block_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,\n    air_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<GkrInputScatterCtx>,\n    num_blocks: u32,\n) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n    check_error(_batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n        block_ctxs.as_ptr(),\n        air_ctxs.as_ptr(),\n        num_blocks,\n    ))\n}\n```\n\n### Change 4: Restructure `log_gkr_input_evals` to separate SCATTER and GLOBAL AIRs\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nAfter building and sorting work_items (line 252), partition by mode:\n\n```rust\n// Separate SCATTER and GLOBAL work items (preserving height-sorted order within each group)\nlet mut scatter_indices: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\nlet mut global_indices: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\nfor (idx, w) in work_items.iter().enumerate() {\n    if w.pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.buffer_size <= 10 {\n        scatter_indices.push(idx);\n    } else {\n        global_indices.push(idx);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBuild SCATTER descriptor arrays (main thread, before spawning workers):\n```rust\n// Build BlockCtx and GkrInputScatterCtx for all SCATTER AIRs\nlet mut block_ctxs: Vec<BlockCtx> = Vec::new();\nlet mut air_ctxs: Vec<GkrInputScatterCtx> = Vec::new();\nfor (air_local_idx, &work_idx) in scatter_indices.iter().enumerate() {\n    let w = &work_items[work_idx];\n    let height = w.air_ctx.height() as u32;\n    let num_air_blocks = height.div_ceil(256);\n\n    for local_block in 0..num_air_blocks {\n        block_ctxs.push(BlockCtx {\n            local_block_idx_x: local_block,\n            air_idx: air_local_idx as u32,\n        });\n    }\n\n    // Build per-AIR context (collecting pointers from existing data structures)\n    air_ctxs.push(GkrInputScatterCtx { /* fields from work item */ });\n}\nlet d_block_ctxs = block_ctxs.to_device()?;\nlet d_air_ctxs = air_ctxs.to_device()?;\nlet total_scatter_blocks = block_ctxs.len() as u32;\n```\n\nModify the multi-threaded section to only process GLOBAL AIRs:\n```rust\n// Adjust num_threads based on GLOBAL count (not total)\nlet mut num_threads = if global_indices.len() >= 100 {\n    NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS.min(global_indices.len())\n} else if global_indices.is_empty() {\n    0\n} else {\n    1\n};\n// ... memory budget check on GLOBAL-only buffer sizes ...\n```\n\n### Change 5: Launch SCATTER batch on background thread\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nInside the `std::thread::scope` block, add a background thread for SCATTER batch:\n\n```rust\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    // Background thread: batched SCATTER evaluation\n    let scatter_handle = if total_scatter_blocks > 0 {\n        Some(s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n            unsafe {\n                batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(\n                    &d_block_ctxs, &d_air_ctxs, total_scatter_blocks\n                )?;\n            }\n            // Lifting for SCATTER AIRs that need it\n            for &work_idx in &scatter_indices {\n                let w = &work_items[work_idx];\n                let height = w.air_ctx.height();\n                let lifted_height = max(height, 1 << w.l_skip);\n                if height != lifted_height {\n                    // Launch per-AIR lifting kernels on this thread's stream\n                    // (sequential, after batch kernel completes)\n                    // ... frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp + frac_matrix_vertically_repeat\n                }\n            }\n            current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n            Ok(())\n        }))\n    } else {\n        None\n    };\n\n    // Worker threads: GLOBAL-mode AIRs only\n    let global_handles: Vec<_> = /* ... existing multi-stream dispatch using global_indices ... */;\n\n    // Join all\n    if let Some(h) = scatter_handle { h.join().unwrap()?; }\n    for h in global_handles { h.join().unwrap()?; }\n    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n})?;\n```\n\n**Borrow checker**: `work_items`, `scatter_indices`, `d_block_ctxs`, `d_air_ctxs` are all borrowed/captured by the scatter thread. Since they're within `thread::scope`, the borrows are safe. `d_block_ctxs` and `d_air_ctxs` are `DeviceBuffer` values that are moved into the scatter thread closure.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Every AIR is processed exactly once. SCATTER AIRs go through the batched kernel; GLOBAL AIRs go through the existing multi-stream path. Both write to the same `leaves` buffer at pre-computed non-overlapping per-AIR offsets.\n\n2. **Output equivalence**: The batched SCATTER kernel uses the same DAG evaluation logic as the existing `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<false>`, just with per-block descriptors instead of per-launch arguments.\n\n3. **Single-thread fallback**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs < 100 threshold), all AIRs are processed sequentially on the main thread (unchanged).\n\n4. **Memory safety**: `GkrInputScatterCtx` pointers reference device data that is valid for the lifetime of `log_gkr_input_evals`. No SCATTER intermediates buffer is needed (local array of 10 elements).\n\n5. **APC 0 no-regression**: Threshold check ensures single-threaded path is used at APC 0.\n\n6. **GLOBAL path unchanged**: GLOBAL-mode AIRs use the existing multi-stream processing with pre-allocated per-thread buffers.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the standard benchmark:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path> --recursion\n```\n\nKey metrics (APC 300):\n- **LogUp GKR**: current ~534ms, expect 10-25ms improvement\n- **STARK excl trace**: current ~1312ms, expect proportional improvement\n- No regression in Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit, Openings\n\nKey metrics (APC 000): must not regress.\n\nRun each configuration at least twice.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of:\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 10ms (median of 2+ runs)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses by more than 20ms\n- Any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 30ms\n- Proof verification fails\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":163,"newStart":1,"newLines":304,"lines":["-# Plan: Increase GKR Input Eval Streams + Round-Robin Distribution","+# Plan: Batch SCATTER-mode GKR Input Evaluation"," "," ## Goal"," ","-Reduce LogUp GKR input evaluation wall-clock time at APC 300 by increasing `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS` from 8 to 16 and switching from contiguous chunk distribution to interleaved (round-robin) work assignment.","+Reduce LogUp GKR input evaluation wall-clock time at APC 300 by batching SCATTER-mode AIR evaluations (buffer_size <= 10) into a single descriptor-array CUDA kernel per segment, launched on a dedicated background thread concurrent with the existing multi-stream GLOBAL-mode processing."," ","-LogUp GKR is the dominant bottleneck at 534ms (40.7% of STARK excl trace). The GKR input eval sub-phase accounts for ~302ms of this across 2 segments (~242ms seg0, ~62ms seg1). The current implementation uses 8 OS threads with contiguous chunking on height-sorted work items, which gives thread 0 all the largest AIRs — worst-case load balance.","+LogUp GKR is 534ms (40.7% of STARK excl trace at APC 300). The GKR input eval sub-phase is ~302ms across 2 segments (~242ms seg0, ~62ms seg1). From nsight profiling: 486 GLOBAL-mode instances (464ms GPU time, median 760us) + 305 SCATTER-mode instances (63ms GPU time, median 111us). SCATTER AIRs each launch a tiny kernel with 4-16 blocks that underutilizes the 128 SMs."," ","-The previous attempt to apply round-robin alone (task `gkr-input-round-robin`) saved only 8ms and was reverted. However, that attempt used 8 threads. The combination of 16 threads (finer work granularity, each thread gets ~20 AIRs instead of ~39) with round-robin distribution has not been tried. The pre-allocated per-thread buffers (from `prealloc-gkr-input-buffers`) eliminate the mutex contention that previously limited thread-count scaling.","+**Key mechanism**: Remove ~150 SCATTER AIRs per segment from the 8 multi-stream worker threads. Each thread processes ~20 GLOBAL-only AIRs instead of ~39 mixed AIRs. The SCATTER batch runs on a 9th background thread, hidden behind the GLOBAL processing. Each GLOBAL worker thread saves ~19 per-AIR overheads: CPU preparation (~25us), kernel launch (~10us), and between-kernel scheduling gap (~50us). Per-thread savings: ~1.6ms. The critical-path thread improves because its total serial work is shorter."," ","+Additionally, the batched SCATTER kernel (one launch, ~1500 blocks) processes all SCATTER AIRs with optimal GPU block scheduling, vs the current approach where tiny 4-16 block kernels leave most SMs idle during each launch.","+"," ## Current Code Path"," "," **File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`"," ","-1. **Constant** (line 30): `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8`","+1. **`log_gkr_input_evals()`** (line 215): Entry point.","+   - Line 232-252: Build `Vec<GkrInputWorkItem>`, sort by descending height","+   - Line 254-279: Compute max buffer sizes for pre-allocation","+   - Line 282: Barrier sync","+   - Line 285-289: Set num_threads (8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1)","+   - Line 291-303: Memory budget check (halve num_threads if pre-alloc > 2GB)","+   - Line 306-321: Pre-allocate per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers`","+   - Line 328-348: Multi-threaded processing: contiguous chunks, all AIR types mixed"," ","-2. **`log_gkr_input_evals()`** (line 215):","-   - Line 232-252: Build work items, sort by descending height","-   - Line 254-279: Compute max buffer sizes across all AIRs","-   - Line 282: Barrier sync (`current_stream_sync`)","-   - Line 285-289: Set `num_threads` = 8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1","-   - Line 291-303: Memory budget check — halve `num_threads` while `num_threads * per_thread_bytes > 2GB`","-   - Line 306-321: Pre-allocate per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers` (intermediates, public_values, partition_ptrs, tmp)","-   - Line 323-327: Single-thread fallback path (num_threads <= 1)","-   - **Line 329**: `let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);` — contiguous chunking","-   - **Line 330-348**: `std::thread::scope` — threads iterate over contiguous `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)`","+2. **`process_gkr_input_air()`** (line 112): Per-AIR processing.","+   - Line 125-129: Build partition pointers from cached_mains + common_main","+   - Line 141-148: Upload public_values to device via `copy_to`","+   - Line 152-156: Upload partition_ptrs to device via `copy_to`","+   - Line 158-160: `is_global = buffer_size > 10` — determines SCATTER vs GLOBAL mode","+   - Line 173-187: Single FFI call: `logup_gkr_input_eval(is_global, ...)`","+   - Line 189-208: Conditional lifting: if height != lifted_height, scale + repeat"," ","-3. **`process_gkr_input_air()`** (line 112):","-   - Per-AIR: collect partition pointers, H2D copy public_values + partition_ptrs, launch `logup_gkr_input_eval` kernel, optional lifting for short traces","-   - Uses pre-allocated thread buffers (no allocation per AIR)","+3. **CUDA kernel**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`","+   - Line 65-208: `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL>` template","+   - SCATTER (GLOBAL=false, line 89-92): `FpExt intermediates[10]` — local array, max 10 nodes","+   - GLOBAL (GLOBAL=true, line 85-87): `d_intermediates` global buffer, stride = task_stride","+   - Line 17-63: `evaluate_dag_entry_gkr()` — switch on SourceInfo.type to load data","+   - Line 94-207: Per-row loop: iterate d_used_nodes, evaluate DAG, write to d_fracs","+   - Line 216-265: Launcher: `grid = kernel_launch_params(is_global ? TASK_SIZE : height, 256)`"," ","-**Why it's slow**: Contiguous chunking on height-sorted items means thread 0 gets the 39 largest AIRs (including several with 5-13ms kernel times). Other threads finish much earlier. The wall-clock is determined by thread 0's total serialized kernel time.","+4. **FFI**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`","+   - Line 278-292: extern declaration","+   - Line 938-968: safe wrapper `logup_gkr_input_eval()`"," ","+**Existing batched pattern reference**: `ZerocheckCtx` (line 48-58 of `cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`) + `BlockCtx` (line 17-22) used by `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel` in `cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`. Each block reads `BlockCtx` to find its AIR, then loads the per-AIR `ZerocheckCtx` struct containing `d_rules`, `d_used_nodes`, `used_nodes_len`, `buffer_size`, etc.","+"," ## Changes"," ","-### Change 1: Increase thread count constant","+### Change 1: Define GkrInputScatterCtx descriptor struct"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, line 30","+**Files**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu` (CUDA) and `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (Rust)"," ","+CUDA side (add to gkr_input.cu, before the launcher function):","+```cuda","+struct GkrInputScatterCtx {","+    FracExt *d_fracs;               // Per-AIR output pointer (into leaves buffer)","+    const Fp *d_preprocessed;       // Per-AIR preprocessed trace (or null)","+    const uint64_t *d_main;         // Per-AIR partition pointers (device array)","+    const Fp *d_public_values;      // Per-AIR public values (or null)","+    const FpExt *d_challenges;      // Shared challenge vector (same for all AIRs)","+    const Rule *d_rules;            // Per-AIR interaction DAG rules","+    const size_t *d_used_nodes;     // Per-AIR used node indices","+    const uint32_t *d_pair_idxs;    // Per-AIR pair index mapping","+    size_t used_nodes_len;          // Per-AIR","+    uint32_t permutation_height;    // Per-AIR trace height","+    uint32_t num_blocks;            // Number of blocks assigned to this AIR","+};","+```","+","+Rust side (add to `cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, near existing context structs):"," ```rust","-// Before:","-const NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8;","+#[repr(C)]","+#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]","+pub struct GkrInputScatterCtx {","+    pub d_fracs: *mut Frac<EF>,","+    pub d_preprocessed: *const F,","+    pub d_main: *const u64,","+    pub d_public_values: *const F,","+    pub d_challenges: *const EF,","+    pub d_rules: *const std::ffi::c_void,","+    pub d_used_nodes: *const usize,","+    pub d_pair_idxs: *const u32,","+    pub used_nodes_len: usize,","+    pub permutation_height: u32,","+    pub num_blocks: u32,","+}","+unsafe impl Send for GkrInputScatterCtx {}","+unsafe impl Sync for GkrInputScatterCtx {}","+```"," ","-// After:","-const NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 16;","+### Change 2: New batched SCATTER kernel","+","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu` (append)","+","+```cuda","+__global__ void batched_evaluate_interactions_scatter_kernel(","+    const BlockCtx *__restrict__ d_block_ctxs,","+    const GkrInputScatterCtx *__restrict__ d_air_ctxs","+) {","+    BlockCtx block_ctx = d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x];","+    GkrInputScatterCtx ctx = d_air_ctxs[block_ctx.air_idx];","+","+    // Per-block task distribution within this AIR","+    uint32_t local_block = block_ctx.local_block_idx_x;","+    uint32_t task_offset = local_block * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;","+    uint32_t task_stride = ctx.num_blocks * blockDim.x;","+","+    FpExt intermediates[10];  // Local array, same as SCATTER mode","+    uint32_t intermediate_stride = 1;","+","+    // Same DAG evaluation loop as existing SCATTER kernel (gkr_input.cu lines 94-207)","+    // using ctx.d_rules, ctx.d_used_nodes, ctx.d_pair_idxs, etc.","+    // Guard: if (task_offset >= ctx.permutation_height) return;","+    // ... (copy evaluation loop body from lines 94-207, replacing","+    //      kernel arguments with ctx.field references)","+}"," ```"," ","-**Why**: More threads provide finer-grained work distribution across the 312 AIRs in APC 300 seg0 (~20 AIRs/thread instead of ~39). Each thread gets fewer large AIRs, reducing the critical-path thread time. Pre-allocated buffers make this viable without mutex contention.","+Launcher:","+```cuda","+extern \"C\" int _batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(","+    const BlockCtx *d_block_ctxs,","+    const GkrInputScatterCtx *d_air_ctxs,","+    uint32_t num_blocks","+) {","+    if (num_blocks == 0) return 0;","+    dim3 grid(num_blocks);","+    dim3 block(256);","+    batched_evaluate_interactions_scatter_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(","+        d_block_ctxs, d_air_ctxs","+    );","+    return CHECK_KERNEL();","+}","+```"," ","-**Memory impact**: At ~100MB per thread buffer set, 16 threads use ~1.6GB vs ~800MB for 8 threads. The memory budget check (line 296) will automatically halve to 8 if per-thread bytes × 16 > 2GB, so this self-corrects if the max buffer size is unusually large.","+### Change 3: Rust FFI + safe wrapper for batched scatter kernel"," ","-### Change 2: Switch from contiguous chunks to round-robin distribution","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, lines 329-348","+Add extern declaration and safe wrapper:","+```rust","+extern \"C\" {","+    fn _batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(","+        block_ctxs: *const BlockCtx,","+        air_ctxs: *const GkrInputScatterCtx,","+        num_blocks: u32,","+    ) -> i32;","+}"," ","-Replace the contiguous `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)` dispatch with interleaved (round-robin) index assignment, following the same pattern used in Round 0 (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1280-1291).","+pub unsafe fn batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(","+    block_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,","+    air_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<GkrInputScatterCtx>,","+    num_blocks: u32,","+) -> Result<(), CudaError> {","+    check_error(_batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(","+        block_ctxs.as_ptr(),","+        air_ctxs.as_ptr(),","+        num_blocks,","+    ))","+}","+```"," ","+### Change 4: Restructure `log_gkr_input_evals` to separate SCATTER and GLOBAL AIRs","+","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`","+","+After building and sorting work_items (line 252), partition by mode:","+"," ```rust","-// Before (lines 329-348):","-let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);","-std::thread::scope(|s| {","-    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers","-        .into_iter()","-        .zip(work_items.chunks(chunk_size))","-        .map(|(mut bufs, chunk)| {","-            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {","-                for w in chunk {","-                    process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?;","-                }","-                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;","-                Ok(())","-            })","-        })","-        .collect();","-    for handle in handles {","-        handle.join().unwrap()?;","+// Separate SCATTER and GLOBAL work items (preserving height-sorted order within each group)","+let mut scatter_indices: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();","+let mut global_indices: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();","+for (idx, w) in work_items.iter().enumerate() {","+    if w.pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.buffer_size <= 10 {","+        scatter_indices.push(idx);","+    } else {","+        global_indices.push(idx);","     }","-    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())","-})?;","+}","+```"," ","-// After:","-// Interleaved (round-robin) assignment on the height-sorted list.","-// Work items are already sorted by descending height. Round-robin","-// distributes them so each thread gets a balanced mix of large and","-// small AIRs (thread 0 gets items 0, N, 2N, ...; thread 1 gets","-// items 1, N+1, 2N+1, ...).","-let mut thread_indices: Vec<Vec<usize>> =","-    (0..num_threads).map(|_| Vec::new()).collect();","-for (idx, _) in work_items.iter().enumerate() {","-    thread_indices[idx % num_threads].push(idx);","+Build SCATTER descriptor arrays (main thread, before spawning workers):","+```rust","+// Build BlockCtx and GkrInputScatterCtx for all SCATTER AIRs","+let mut block_ctxs: Vec<BlockCtx> = Vec::new();","+let mut air_ctxs: Vec<GkrInputScatterCtx> = Vec::new();","+for (air_local_idx, &work_idx) in scatter_indices.iter().enumerate() {","+    let w = &work_items[work_idx];","+    let height = w.air_ctx.height() as u32;","+    let num_air_blocks = height.div_ceil(256);","+","+    for local_block in 0..num_air_blocks {","+        block_ctxs.push(BlockCtx {","+            local_block_idx_x: local_block,","+            air_idx: air_local_idx as u32,","+        });","+    }","+","+    // Build per-AIR context (collecting pointers from existing data structures)","+    air_ctxs.push(GkrInputScatterCtx { /* fields from work item */ });"," }","+let d_block_ctxs = block_ctxs.to_device()?;","+let d_air_ctxs = air_ctxs.to_device()?;","+let total_scatter_blocks = block_ctxs.len() as u32;","+```"," ","+Modify the multi-threaded section to only process GLOBAL AIRs:","+```rust","+// Adjust num_threads based on GLOBAL count (not total)","+let mut num_threads = if global_indices.len() >= 100 {","+    NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS.min(global_indices.len())","+} else if global_indices.is_empty() {","+    0","+} else {","+    1","+};","+// ... memory budget check on GLOBAL-only buffer sizes ...","+```","+","+### Change 5: Launch SCATTER batch on background thread","+","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`","+","+Inside the `std::thread::scope` block, add a background thread for SCATTER batch:","+","+```rust"," std::thread::scope(|s| {","-    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers","-        .into_iter()","-        .zip(thread_indices.into_iter())","-        .map(|(mut bufs, indices)| {","-            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {","-                for &idx in &indices {","-                    process_gkr_input_air(&work_items[idx], &mut bufs)?;","+    // Background thread: batched SCATTER evaluation","+    let scatter_handle = if total_scatter_blocks > 0 {","+        Some(s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {","+            unsafe {","+                batched_gkr_input_eval_scatter(","+                    &d_block_ctxs, &d_air_ctxs, total_scatter_blocks","+                )?;","+            }","+            // Lifting for SCATTER AIRs that need it","+            for &work_idx in &scatter_indices {","+                let w = &work_items[work_idx];","+                let height = w.air_ctx.height();","+                let lifted_height = max(height, 1 << w.l_skip);","+                if height != lifted_height {","+                    // Launch per-AIR lifting kernels on this thread's stream","+                    // (sequential, after batch kernel completes)","+                    // ... frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp + frac_matrix_vertically_repeat","                 }","-                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;","-                Ok(())","-            })","-        })","-        .collect();","-    for handle in handles {","-        handle.join().unwrap()?;","-    }","+            }","+            current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;","+            Ok(())","+        }))","+    } else {","+        None","+    };","+","+    // Worker threads: GLOBAL-mode AIRs only","+    let global_handles: Vec<_> = /* ... existing multi-stream dispatch using global_indices ... */;","+","+    // Join all","+    if let Some(h) = scatter_handle { h.join().unwrap()?; }","+    for h in global_handles { h.join().unwrap()?; }","     Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())"," })?;"," ```"," ","-**Why**: Round-robin on height-sorted items ensures each thread gets every Nth AIR by descending height. Thread 0 gets the 1st, 17th, 33rd, ... largest AIRs (with 16 threads). This distributes both large and small AIRs evenly, balancing GPU kernel time and per-AIR fixed overhead across threads.","+**Borrow checker**: `work_items`, `scatter_indices`, `d_block_ctxs`, `d_air_ctxs` are all borrowed/captured by the scatter thread. Since they're within `thread::scope`, the borrows are safe. `d_block_ctxs` and `d_air_ctxs` are `DeviceBuffer` values that are moved into the scatter thread closure."," ","-**Borrow checker note**: The `work_items` slice must be accessible from all threads. Since `thread::scope` guarantees the spawned threads don't outlive the scope, `&work_items[idx]` borrows are safe. The `thread_indices` Vec is moved into the iterator, and each thread gets ownership of its `Vec<usize>`.","-"," ## Invariants"," ","-1. **Correctness**: Every work item is processed exactly once. Round-robin assignment is a partition of indices `0..work_items.len()` across `num_threads` threads. No work item is skipped or duplicated.","+1. **Correctness**: Every AIR is processed exactly once. SCATTER AIRs go through the batched kernel; GLOBAL AIRs go through the existing multi-stream path. Both write to the same `leaves` buffer at pre-computed non-overlapping per-AIR offsets."," ","-2. **Output equivalence**: Each thread writes to its work items' pre-computed `leaves_ptr` offsets (set during Phase 1). These offsets are per-AIR and non-overlapping. The output `leaves` buffer is identical regardless of thread assignment order.","+2. **Output equivalence**: The batched SCATTER kernel uses the same DAG evaluation logic as the existing `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<false>`, just with per-block descriptors instead of per-launch arguments."," ","-3. **Single-thread fallback**: The `num_threads <= 1` path (line 323-327) is unchanged. AIR count < 100 still uses single-threaded sequential processing.","+3. **Single-thread fallback**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs < 100 threshold), all AIRs are processed sequentially on the main thread (unchanged)."," ","-4. **Memory budget**: The existing budget check (line 296) automatically halves `num_threads` if total pre-allocation exceeds 2GB. With 16 threads at ~100MB each = 1.6GB, this check passes. If a future workload has larger buffers, it degrades gracefully to 8 or fewer threads.","+4. **Memory safety**: `GkrInputScatterCtx` pointers reference device data that is valid for the lifetime of `log_gkr_input_evals`. No SCATTER intermediates buffer is needed (local array of 10 elements)."," ","-5. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs < 100 threshold), the single-threaded path is taken. No change in behavior.","+5. **APC 0 no-regression**: Threshold check ensures single-threaded path is used at APC 0."," ","+6. **GLOBAL path unchanged**: GLOBAL-mode AIRs use the existing multi-stream processing with pre-allocated per-thread buffers.","+"," ## Measurement Plan"," ","-Run the standard benchmark for APC 300 and APC 000:","-","+Run the standard benchmark:"," ```bash"," cd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing","-/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <output_path> --recursion","+/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path> --recursion"," ```"," ","-Analyze with spec.py:","-```bash","-python3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>","-```","+Key metrics (APC 300):","+- **LogUp GKR**: current ~534ms, expect 10-25ms improvement","+- **STARK excl trace**: current ~1312ms, expect proportional improvement","+- No regression in Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit, Openings"," ","-**Key metrics to compare (APC 300)**:","-- LogUp GKR: baseline 534ms, current ~534ms, expect improvement","-- STARK excl trace: baseline 2455ms, current ~1312ms, expect improvement","-- No regression in other phases (Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit, Openings)","+Key metrics (APC 000): must not regress."," ","-**Key metrics to compare (APC 000)**:","-- STARK excl trace: must not regress (current ~2179ms)","-- The single-threaded fallback ensures no change at APC 0","+Run each configuration at least twice."," ","-**Per-segment GKR input eval timing**: Check the `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals` tracing span in the benchmark output for per-segment breakdown.","-","-Run each configuration at least twice to account for measurement noise.","-"," ## Rollback Criteria"," ","-Revert if ANY of these conditions hold:","-- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 15ms (median of 2+ runs)","+Revert if ANY of:","+- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 10ms (median of 2+ runs)"," - STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses by more than 20ms"," - Any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 30ms","-- Any correctness failure (proof verification fails)","+- Proof verification fails"]}],"originalFile":"# Plan: Increase GKR Input Eval Streams + Round-Robin Distribution\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce LogUp GKR input evaluation wall-clock time at APC 300 by increasing `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS` from 8 to 16 and switching from contiguous chunk distribution to interleaved (round-robin) work assignment.\n\nLogUp GKR is the dominant bottleneck at 534ms (40.7% of STARK excl trace). The GKR input eval sub-phase accounts for ~302ms of this across 2 segments (~242ms seg0, ~62ms seg1). The current implementation uses 8 OS threads with contiguous chunking on height-sorted work items, which gives thread 0 all the largest AIRs — worst-case load balance.\n\nThe previous attempt to apply round-robin alone (task `gkr-input-round-robin`) saved only 8ms and was reverted. However, that attempt used 8 threads. The combination of 16 threads (finer work granularity, each thread gets ~20 AIRs instead of ~39) with round-robin distribution has not been tried. The pre-allocated per-thread buffers (from `prealloc-gkr-input-buffers`) eliminate the mutex contention that previously limited thread-count scaling.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\n1. **Constant** (line 30): `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8`\n\n2. **`log_gkr_input_evals()`** (line 215):\n   - Line 232-252: Build work items, sort by descending height\n   - Line 254-279: Compute max buffer sizes across all AIRs\n   - Line 282: Barrier sync (`current_stream_sync`)\n   - Line 285-289: Set `num_threads` = 8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1\n   - Line 291-303: Memory budget check — halve `num_threads` while `num_threads * per_thread_bytes > 2GB`\n   - Line 306-321: Pre-allocate per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers` (intermediates, public_values, partition_ptrs, tmp)\n   - Line 323-327: Single-thread fallback path (num_threads <= 1)\n   - **Line 329**: `let chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);` — contiguous chunking\n   - **Line 330-348**: `std::thread::scope` — threads iterate over contiguous `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)`\n\n3. **`process_gkr_input_air()`** (line 112):\n   - Per-AIR: collect partition pointers, H2D copy public_values + partition_ptrs, launch `logup_gkr_input_eval` kernel, optional lifting for short traces\n   - Uses pre-allocated thread buffers (no allocation per AIR)\n\n**Why it's slow**: Contiguous chunking on height-sorted items means thread 0 gets the 39 largest AIRs (including several with 5-13ms kernel times). Other threads finish much earlier. The wall-clock is determined by thread 0's total serialized kernel time.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Increase thread count constant\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, line 30\n\n```rust\n// Before:\nconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8;\n\n// After:\nconst NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 16;\n```\n\n**Why**: More threads provide finer-grained work distribution across the 312 AIRs in APC 300 seg0 (~20 AIRs/thread instead of ~39). Each thread gets fewer large AIRs, reducing the critical-path thread time. Pre-allocated buffers make this viable without mutex contention.\n\n**Memory impact**: At ~100MB per thread buffer set, 16 threads use ~1.6GB vs ~800MB for 8 threads. The memory budget check (line 296) will automatically halve to 8 if per-thread bytes × 16 > 2GB, so this self-corrects if the max buffer size is unusually large.\n\n### Change 2: Switch from contiguous chunks to round-robin distribution\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, lines 329-348\n\nReplace the contiguous `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)` dispatch with interleaved (round-robin) index assignment, following the same pattern used in Round 0 (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1280-1291).\n\n```rust\n// Before (lines 329-348):\nlet chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n        .into_iter()\n        .zip(work_items.chunks(chunk_size))\n        .map(|(mut bufs, chunk)| {\n            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n                for w in chunk {\n                    process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?;\n                }\n                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n                Ok(())\n            })\n        })\n        .collect();\n    for handle in handles {\n        handle.join().unwrap()?;\n    }\n    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n})?;\n\n// After:\n// Interleaved (round-robin) assignment on the height-sorted list.\n// Work items are already sorted by descending height. Round-robin\n// distributes them so each thread gets a balanced mix of large and\n// small AIRs (thread 0 gets items 0, N, 2N, ...; thread 1 gets\n// items 1, N+1, 2N+1, ...).\nlet mut thread_indices: Vec<Vec<usize>> =\n    (0..num_threads).map(|_| Vec::new()).collect();\nfor (idx, _) in work_items.iter().enumerate() {\n    thread_indices[idx % num_threads].push(idx);\n}\n\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n        .into_iter()\n        .zip(thread_indices.into_iter())\n        .map(|(mut bufs, indices)| {\n            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n                for &idx in &indices {\n                    process_gkr_input_air(&work_items[idx], &mut bufs)?;\n                }\n                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n                Ok(())\n            })\n        })\n        .collect();\n    for handle in handles {\n        handle.join().unwrap()?;\n    }\n    Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n})?;\n```\n\n**Why**: Round-robin on height-sorted items ensures each thread gets every Nth AIR by descending height. Thread 0 gets the 1st, 17th, 33rd, ... largest AIRs (with 16 threads). This distributes both large and small AIRs evenly, balancing GPU kernel time and per-AIR fixed overhead across threads.\n\n**Borrow checker note**: The `work_items` slice must be accessible from all threads. Since `thread::scope` guarantees the spawned threads don't outlive the scope, `&work_items[idx]` borrows are safe. The `thread_indices` Vec is moved into the iterator, and each thread gets ownership of its `Vec<usize>`.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Every work item is processed exactly once. Round-robin assignment is a partition of indices `0..work_items.len()` across `num_threads` threads. No work item is skipped or duplicated.\n\n2. **Output equivalence**: Each thread writes to its work items' pre-computed `leaves_ptr` offsets (set during Phase 1). These offsets are per-AIR and non-overlapping. The output `leaves` buffer is identical regardless of thread assignment order.\n\n3. **Single-thread fallback**: The `num_threads <= 1` path (line 323-327) is unchanged. AIR count < 100 still uses single-threaded sequential processing.\n\n4. **Memory budget**: The existing budget check (line 296) automatically halves `num_threads` if total pre-allocation exceeds 2GB. With 16 threads at ~100MB each = 1.6GB, this check passes. If a future workload has larger buffers, it degrades gracefully to 8 or fewer threads.\n\n5. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs < 100 threshold), the single-threaded path is taken. No change in behavior.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the standard benchmark for APC 300 and APC 000:\n\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n/home/georg/powdr/target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <output_path> --recursion\n```\n\nAnalyze with spec.py:\n```bash\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>\n```\n\n**Key metrics to compare (APC 300)**:\n- LogUp GKR: baseline 534ms, current ~534ms, expect improvement\n- STARK excl trace: baseline 2455ms, current ~1312ms, expect improvement\n- No regression in other phases (Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit, Openings)\n\n**Key metrics to compare (APC 000)**:\n- STARK excl trace: must not regress (current ~2179ms)\n- The single-threaded fallback ensures no change at APC 0\n\n**Per-segment GKR input eval timing**: Check the `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals` tracing span in the benchmark output for per-segment breakdown.\n\nRun each configuration at least twice to account for measurement noise.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of these conditions hold:\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by less than 15ms (median of 2+ runs)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 regresses by more than 20ms\n- Any APC 0 metric regresses by more than 30ms\n- Any correctness failure (proof verification fails)\n"}}
